window.GUIDE = {"groups": [{"id": "start", "label": "Start here"}, {"id": "foundations", "label": "Foundations"}, {"id": "technical", "label": "Technical skills"}, {"id": "cognitive", "label": "Cognitive skills"}, {"id": "behavioural", "label": "Behavioural skills"}, {"id": "reference", "label": "Reference"}], "pages": [{"id": "welcome", "label": "Welcome", "group": "start", "title": "Principles of Human Factors", "html": "<p class=\"subtitle\" id=\"b1\">App and course written and designed by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu</p><figure class=\"fig portrait\" id=\"b3\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image1.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: staff and a patient talking in a bright hospital waiting area.\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1536\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b6\">Course philosophy</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b7\">This course is for everyone, no matter what role or seniority they have in the organisation</li><li id=\"b8\">It focuses on teaching people a common language of safety</li><li id=\"b9\">It focuses on simple and practical concepts, rather than theory</li><li id=\"b10\">It offers tools that you can use to improve your performance in your professional or personal life.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b12\">How is the material built?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b13\">Simple: Plain language, no jargon for its own sake.</li><li id=\"b14\">At scale: Built to reach a whole organisation, not a select few.</li><li id=\"b15\">Role-agnostic: Applies to every job in the building, clinical and non-clinical</li><li id=\"b16\">Micro-learning: Ten-minute chunks. Read one topic at a time, in your own time.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b18\">Message to the user:</h3><p class=\"pull\" id=\"b19\">“To err is human”</p><p id=\"b20\">Error is part of the human condition. It’s how we avoid, trap and mitigate risks and errors that defines our performance and the safety of our organisation.</p><blockquote class=\"quote\" id=\"b21\"><p>\"Human factors are an operator's manual to the human.\"</p></blockquote><h3 id=\"b23\">Source Material</h3><p id=\"b24\">All content is derived from:</p><p id=\"b25\"><i>P Gogalniceanu</i>. “<b>High Performance in Healthcare - <i>Optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery”</i></b>, <i>King’s College London, 2019</i></p>"}, {"id": "how-to-use", "label": "How to use this guide", "group": "start", "title": "1. How to use this guide", "html": "<p class=\"kicker\" id=\"b30\"><i>Orientation</i></p><p id=\"b31\"><i>Welcome to the Principles of Human Factors App</i><b>. </b>This educational platform was developed by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu for the Simulation and Interactive Learning (SaIL) Centre at Guy’s and St.Thomas NHS Foundation Trust.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b33\"><b> There is no fixed order, and nothing is locked. Open what you need, use it, and close it.</b></p><h3 id=\"b34\">Three ways in</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b35\">Search - press / from anywhere and type a word: fatigue, checklist, bias. Search covers every topic, tool and glossary entry.</li><li id=\"b36\">Index - the left-hand list is grouped the way the subject is: foundations, then technical, cognitive and behavioural skills.</li><li id=\"b37\">Toolkits - every practical tool in the guide, pulled onto one page. Start here if you have a problem in front of you right now.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b39\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">What gets recorded</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b40\">Nothing. This page does not track which topics you have opened - there is no progress to pick up and no test to pass. Open what you need.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "aims", "label": "Aims, objectives and outcomes", "group": "start", "title": "2. Aims, objectives and outcomes", "html": "<h2 id=\"b44\">Aim</h2><p id=\"b45\">This course and App aim to introduce participants to the <b>principles</b> and <b>common language </b>of human factors, in order to facilitate patient safety and operational efficiency across GSTT.</p><h3 id=\"b46\">Learning Objectives</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b47\">To define what human factors (HF) are and how they work</li><li id=\"b48\">To introduce participants to basic HF concepts</li><li id=\"b49\">To develop a knowledge of how technical, cognitive and behavioural skills can be used to create safety and security</li></ul>"}, {"id": "performance", "label": "Human performance", "group": "foundations", "title": "3. Human Performance and Course Structure", "html": "<h2 id=\"b53\">What affects human performance?</h2><p id=\"b54\">Five factors affect human performance. These give rise to both quality and safety, as well as risk, threats and errors:</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b55\" class=\"l1\">Technical and non-technical (human factors) skills of the operator</li><li id=\"b56\" class=\"l1\">Operator condition: physical, cognitive and emotional.</li><li id=\"b57\" class=\"l1\">Culture (personal and organisational): values, attitudes, habits and identities.</li><li id=\"b58\" class=\"l1\">Systems: the people, places, tools and rules available to do work.</li><li id=\"b59\" class=\"l1\">Task: complexity, magnitude and urgency</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b61\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image2.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: technical, cognitive and behavioural skills pass through operator condition, culture and health systems.\" width=\"1261\" height=\"928\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure>"}, {"id": "operator", "label": "Operator condition", "group": "foundations", "title": "4. Operator (biological) condition", "html": "<h3 id=\"b66\">Operator condition:</h3><p id=\"b67\">We can only perform tasks to the extent our biological condition allows.</p><p id=\"b68\">We all have limitations which are impacted by three factors:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b69\"><b>Physical status</b>: tiredness, dehydration, hunger, medical conditions, medications, etc.</li><li id=\"b70\"><b>Cognitive status</b>:  cognitive overload (thinking about too many things or thinking for too long) - or its opposite, monotony (boredom).</li><li id=\"b71\"><b>Emotional status</b>: stress, anger, fear or moral injury.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b73\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Human factors and aviation accidents</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p class=\"stat\" id=\"b74\">70–80% of civil and military aviation accidents have been reported to involve human error.</p><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b75\">References: Wiegmann DA, Shappell SA. A human error analysis of commercial aviation accidents using the Human Factors Analysis and Classification System (HFACS). Washington (DC): Federal Aviation Administration, Office of Aviation Medicine; 2001. Report No.: DOT/FAA/AM-01/3. Available from: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf</a>.</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b77\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Human factors and clinical error</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b79\">In one Dutch study of 21 hospitals, human causes contributed to <b>61% </b>of adverse events.</p><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b80\">Smits M, Zegers M, Groenewegen PP, Timmermans DRM, Zwaan L, van der Wal G, et al. Exploring the causes of adverse events in hospitals and potential prevention strategies. Qual Saf Health Care. 2010;19(5):e5. doi:10.1136/qshc.2008.030726.</p></div></aside><h3 id=\"b82\">Deconditioning in healthcare</h3><h3 id=\"b83\">What physical factors can impair safe performance?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b84\">Night shifts / Long shifts</li><li id=\"b85\">Long commutes</li><li id=\"b86\">Operations that overrun</li><li id=\"b87\">Working with no natural light</li><li id=\"b88\">No access to rest areas</li><li id=\"b89\">No water or healthy food</li><li id=\"b90\">Medical problems or medications</li></ul><h3 id=\"b92\">What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b93\">Distractions</li><li id=\"b94\">Multi-tasking</li><li id=\"b95\">Working overtime</li><li id=\"b96\">Weekend work</li></ul><h3 id=\"b98\">What emotional factors can impair safe performance?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b99\">Working in isolated or high-risk roles</li><li id=\"b100\">Conflict and work politics</li><li id=\"b101\">Family instability</li></ul><p id=\"b103\">If you are exposed to any of the above risk factors – beware! You may be at risk of making an error.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b105\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image3.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: a tired night-shift worker hurrying down a corridor carrying a stack of paperwork.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b107\">What fatigue and stress do to performance?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b108\">Increased reaction time</li><li id=\"b109\">Reduced attentiveness</li><li id=\"b110\">Impaired memory</li><li id=\"b111\">Withdrawn mood</li><li id=\"b112\">Loss of empathy</li><li id=\"b113\">Risk-taking behaviour</li><li id=\"b114\">Incivility or conflict-generating behaviour</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b116\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Good to know about fatigue…</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b117\">Every item on that list can be easily missed by the person experiencing it. Fatigue degrades the very faculty you would use to notice you are fatigued. This is why you need to think ahead, take feedback from colleagues, set boundaries, have guardrails and rely on systems being put in place.</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b119\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">3 Principles for optimising operator condition</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b120\">You have to be ‘fit for the job’. Optimise yourself to optimise your outcomes. Think body, mind and emotions.</li><li id=\"b121\">Never do safety-critical work if you are acutely unwell or have not slept.</li><li id=\"b122\">Manage your energy: eat, sleep, hydrate, move, socialise.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"id": "startle", "label": "Startle syndrome", "group": "foundations", "title": "What is ‘Startle Syndrome’?", "html": "<ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b125\">Our ability to think calmly and rationally is the first we lose when we are faced with a surprising (‘startling’) or threatening event.</li><li id=\"b126\">There is nothing wrong or shameful about this. It happens to all of us. It’s part of the human defence reaction.</li><li id=\"b127\">When threatened, we switch from logical to emotional thinking - that is just how our brains are wired.</li><li id=\"b128\">This phenomenon has different names: “the fog of war”, “the red mist” or the “amygdala hijack”</li><li id=\"b129\">This is good to know, because it predicts how we respond to a sudden emergency – and it’s not always in a good way!</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b131\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Emotions beat reason every time. </h3><div class=\"co-body\"></div></aside><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b132\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image4.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: a startled surgeon holding a clipboard in an operating theatre.\" width=\"1138\" height=\"762\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b134\">The startled human:</h3><p id=\"b135\">Startle is a reaction where your emotions take over your thinking. You stop being rational and logical. Instead, you will display one of four (dangerous) fear or threat reactions:</p><p id=\"b136\">1. <b>Fight</b> - impulsive action or aggressive behaviour. Doing something just for the sake of doing it.</p><p id=\"b137\">2. <b>Flight</b> - leaving or withdrawing - physically or by handing the problem to someone else.</p><p id=\"b138\">3. <b>Freeze</b> - inaction. You just stop functioning or engaging.</p><p id=\"b139\">4. <b>Fawn</b> - trying to appease others rather than advocating your point of view, speaking truth to power or standing up for yourself.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b141\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image5.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: The startled human - fight, flight, freeze and fawn.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b142\"><b>Remember: </b>Being affected by the startle syndrome is not a character flaw - it’s part of our deep-seated behavioural code.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b143\">Our job is to recognise startle and control it as best as we can. We will not always be successful.</li><li id=\"b144\">This matters when we observe sudden or atypical unprofessional behaviours. In such cases, we must ask – “Are we not just seeing a startled or stressed individual?” “Are there stressors, threats or fears that are driving these unusual behaviours?”</li><li id=\"b145\">It’s critical we differentiate between the startle effect and underperformance.</li><li id=\"b146\">This does not mean we justify or tolerate incivility or unprofessionalism.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b148\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Startle-management toolkit</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b149\">How do you manage your startle syndrome?</p><h4 id=\"b150\">Startle Tool 1: ‘Stop and Sit on your hands”</h4><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b151\">“Sit on your hands”. Fight the impulse to jump to solutions. The first instinct is rarely the right one.</li><li id=\"b152\">Stop - do nothing for a moment. Very few emergencies are made worse by three seconds of doing nothing.</li><li id=\"b153\">Distract the distraction: count to 30, take 10 deep breaths, make a hot drink, go for a walk, play with your watch.</li><li id=\"b154\">Stay calm and methodical: Re-orient using a structure you already know by heart.</li></ol><h4 id=\"b157\">Startle Tool 2: Have a mental model or cognitive re-orientation tool you can fall back on.</h4><p id=\"b159\">Some examples of mental models or tools to re-orient in a startled situation:</p><h4 id=\"b160\">ABCD (Medical)</h4><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b161\">Airway</li><li id=\"b162\">Breathing</li><li id=\"b163\">Circulation</li><li id=\"b164\">Disability</li></ul><h4 id=\"b166\">ABCD (Surgical)</h4><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b167\">Access</li><li id=\"b168\">Bleeding control</li><li id=\"b169\">Contamination/infection control</li><li id=\"b170\">Decide: damage control vs definitive care</li><li id=\"b171\">Exit (damage control) or Escalate (get help in definitive care)</li></ul><h4 id=\"b173\">PMC</h4><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b174\">Priority actions: critical tasks</li><li id=\"b175\">Move: move people, mobilise resources, modify environments</li><li id=\"b176\">Communicate</li></ul><h4 id=\"b178\">4Ps</h4><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b179\">Patient</li><li id=\"b180\">Place</li><li id=\"b181\">Procedure</li><li id=\"b182\">Personnel (staff)</li></ul></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b184\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Cognitive tools exist not just to aid diagnosis and provide initial medical care. They provide the startled brain a familiar rail to run on until it can think logically again.</h3><div class=\"co-body\"></div></aside>"}, {"id": "culture", "label": "Culture", "group": "foundations", "title": "5. Culture", "html": "<h3 id=\"b188\">What is culture?</h3><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b189\">Culture refers to the values, attitudes and habits that shape how we feel, think and behave.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b190\">We acquire culture from the social environment we grow up in, live in and work in.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b191\">Often, we are unaware of how our culture affects us.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b193\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image6.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: three colleagues walking and talking through a colourful town.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b195\">Culture determines how we answer the following questions:</h3><p id=\"b196\">What is normal to you?</p><p id=\"b197\">What does good look like?</p><p id=\"b198\">How should things be done?</p><h3 id=\"b200\">The problem with culture</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b201\">It cannot be seen.</li><li id=\"b202\">It cannot be measured.</li><li id=\"b203\">It cannot be changed easily.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b205\">What determines culture?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b206\">Family</li><li id=\"b207\">Social group</li><li id=\"b208\">Professional group</li><li id=\"b209\">Ethnicity</li><li id=\"b210\">Nationality</li><li id=\"b211\">Our attitudes to continuous learning and self-growth</li></ul><h3 id=\"b213\">What actually changes culture?</h3><h3 id=\"b214\">The three Cs</h3><p id=\"b215\"><b>1. Catastrophes</b> - shocks and harmful events. This is an effective way to change culture, but it is also the worst - because it comes at great cost.</p><p id=\"b216\"><b>2. Champions</b> - credible role models who lead by example.</p><p id=\"b217\"><b>3. Case studies </b> - storytelling. Humans are emotional before they are rational. A well-told story changes how we feel, and therefore how we think, decide, and behave. A story can change what data, reason, and logic often can’t.</p><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b219\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Titanic (1912)</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b220\">A shock large enough to rewrite maritime rules: lifeboats for all on board, continuous radio watch, an ice patrol. Nothing about the safety culture was new that night. The catastrophe simply made the existing arguments impossible to ignore.</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b222\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Rosa Parks (1955)</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b223\">On 1 December 1955, Rosa Parks, a seamstress and experienced civil rights activist, refused an order to give up her seat to a passenger on a segregated bus in Montgomery, Alabama. Her arrest helped spark the Montgomery bus boycott: 381 days of collective action that challenged racial segregation. Her courage became a catalyst for change because an organised community rallied behind it.</p><p id=\"b224\">Champions change culture by challenging accepted norms, having the courage to go against the grain and giving others the confidence to act. Lasting change comes when that example becomes a shared commitment, supported by collective action.</p></div></aside><h3 id=\"b226\">A few cultures you should know about:</h3><p id=\"b228\"><b>Culture of safety</b> - “Safety first” - first do no harm; be safe and secure in everything you do.</p><p id=\"b230\"><b>Culture of learning</b> – ‘Every day is a school day’. We need to be curious: learn from the past, keep up with current innovations, and think about how we can improve the future.</p><p id=\"b232\"><b>Culture of resilience</b> - “Failures will happen - better be ready to prevent, respond and recover.”</p><p id=\"b234\"><b>Culture of justice</b> - When failure occurs, draw a line between acceptable and unacceptable behaviour.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b235\">Was this bad luck, a genuinely innocent mistake or reckless behaviour?</li><li id=\"b236\">Only then can you decide the outcome: disciplinary action, training, or support.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b239\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">A <b>just culture</b> is not a <b>“no-blame culture”</b>.</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b240\">We must all be responsible and accountable.</li></ul></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b242\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Just Culture</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b243\">When an unintended outcome occurs, we need to understand why.</p><p id=\"b245\">This helps us learn and avoid it happening again. It’s about safety, not blame. The next person to be harmed could be you, me or someone we care about.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b247\">Justice arises from the fair attribution of responsibility rather than looking for someone to blame.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b249\">It is important to first ask <b>“Why did this happen?”</b> rather than <b>“Who did it?”</b></p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b251\">Only then can we debrief fairly, transparently and openly in order to learn.</li><li id=\"b254\">A just culture does not mean we always find systems and circumstances to blame instead of holding individuals accountable.</li><li id=\"b255\">A just culture asks a prior question - was this a genuine error, a risky choice, or reckless behaviour? - and applies a different response to each.</li><li id=\"b256\">Staff will report threats and errors only when they know they will be treated fairly and trust a system that holds people accountable when it should.</li></ul><p id=\"b258\">In a just culture, accountability is attributed to individuals fairly and proportionally.</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b259\">In an innocent mistake, such as a system failure or misunderstanding, support is provided to the individual involved</li><li id=\"b260\">In an error of inexperience, lack of skill or knowledge, training or coaching is provided</li><li id=\"b261\">If the error occurred due to negligence, inappropriate risk-taking behaviour or wilful misdoing, appropriate disciplinary action may be considered.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"id": "systems", "label": "Health systems", "group": "foundations", "title": "6. Health Systems", "html": "<h3 id=\"b265\">Key question: What makes up a health system?</h3><p id=\"b266\">Humans do not perform tasks in isolation. They perform in specific work environments, also known as ecosystems.</p><p id=\"b268\">A health system consists of four elements:</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b269\"><b>People</b> – the humans carrying out the tasks</li><li id=\"b271\"><b>Places</b> – the physical environment where work is carried out (e.g. the ward, the clinic, operating theatre or GP practice)</li><li id=\"b273\"><b>Tools</b> – the equipment and technology that carry out the work. These include both hardware and software. Examples include computer stations, medical devices, electronic medical records, funding, and other assets or resources.</li><li id=\"b275\"><b>Rules </b>- the policies, protocols, procedures and playbooks that guide how we work; this can also be defined as the ‘organisational’ environment</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b276\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image7.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: a health system made of people, places, tools and rules.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b277\">Threats, errors and harm usually arise from one of the four categories mentioned above: <b><i>people, places, tools and rules</i></b>.</p><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b279\">Reference: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">How crises work: A model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">International Journal of Surgery </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">104:p</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> 106711, August 2022. </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">P </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">Gogalniceanu</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> et al</a></p>"}, {"id": "crises", "label": "Threats, errors, harm and crises", "group": "foundations", "title": "Threats, Errors, Harm and Crises", "html": "<p id=\"b282\"><b>Key question:</b> Is “zero harm” an aspiration or a realistic goal?</p><h3 id=\"b284\">How does harm occur?</h3><p id=\"b285\">Harm is not always an accident.</p><p id=\"b286\">Harm occurs through a sequence of events, known as the ‘crisis chain’</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b287\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image8.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the crisis chain - threats lead to errors, errors to harm, harm to crisis.\" width=\"1274\" height=\"444\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b289\"><b>Threats</b> are any risk factors that make errors more likely. Threats are conditions that push towards failure. Examples:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b290\">People: tired, untrained or unwell staff</li><li id=\"b291\">Places: a ward that has no clean water supply</li><li id=\"b292\">Tools: faulty infusion pumps or X-ray equipment</li><li id=\"b293\">Rules: rotas that do not allow sufficient rest times between shifts</li></ul><p id=\"b295\"><b>Errors</b> are unintended outcomes – also known as system ‘failures’.  Errors themselves don’t necessarily lead to harm. Errors can be either inconsequential (nothing bad happens) or consequential (something bad happens). However, if you do nothing about an error, it may lead to harm the next time it happens.</p><p id=\"b297\"><b>Harm</b> describes errors that negatively impact someone or something, for example, a patient, a member of staff, or the organisation.</p><p id=\"b299\"><b>Crises</b> are situations where harms have not been correctly mitigated. This creates novel, uncertain, time-sensitive, and safety-critical situations. If not managed appropriately, crises can have catastrophic or terminal outcomes. Crises are characterised by multiple competing priorities, making the problem and solution dynamic and unclear.</p><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b300\">Reference: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"><b>How crises work: A model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. </b></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"><i>International Journal of Surgery</i></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">104:p</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> 106711, August 2022.</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> P </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\">Gogalniceanu</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/international-journal-of-surgery/fulltext/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711~how-crises-work-a-model-of-error-cause-and-effect-in\"> et al</a></p><h3 id=\"b302\">Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?</h3><p id=\"b303\"><b>Health systems are tightly coupled</b> - meaning they contain many moving parts that are interconnected and interdependent.</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b304\">A tightly coupled system has little slack</li><li id=\"b305\">It is routinely exposed to multiple threats</li><li id=\"b306\">It undertakes complex tasks</li><li id=\"b307\">A change in one part can impact every other part</li><li id=\"b308\">This means it is easy to initiate a crisis chain in healthcare</li></ol><p id=\"b309\">Because we work in complex systems, we can't eliminate harm completely. Instead, we should aim to prevent it, respond to it, reduce its impact and recover from it to create even safer operators and systems.</p><p id=\"b310\">Getting things right the first time is therefore essential</p><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b312\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Safety mindset</b> - Errors happen all the time. It’s not a matter of <b>if</b>, it is a matter of <b>when</b> a major failure will occur. Are you ready for it? What would you need to change or prepare right now, if today was the day? Always ask, ‘what if?’.</h3><div class=\"co-body\"></div></aside><h3 id=\"b314\">How do we model and analyse error?</h3><h3 id=\"b315\">Bow-tie analysis:</h3><p id=\"b316\">A bow-tie analysis is a simple way of looking at the cause and effect of any hazard or failure - what leads to an error on one side, and what is the impact (harms) on the other.</p><figure class=\"fig bowtie bowtie-a\" id=\"b317\"><div class=\"fig-in\"><img src=\"img/image9.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: bow-tie - threats lead to an error, which leads to harms; prevention before, recovery after.\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"bt-arrow\">Risk<span>x</span></span><span class=\"bt-crisis\"><span>C</span><span>R</span><span>I</span><span>S</span><span>I</span><span>S</span></span></div></figure><p class=\"caption\" id=\"b318\">Diagram: Bow-tie: threats and traps, barriers, error, mitigations, harms</p><h4 id=\"b322\">Managing Crises:</h4><p id=\"b324\"><b>Barriers:</b> Anything that stops or avoids a threat from becoming an error: a checklist, a second operator, a design that prevents wrong or incorrect use.</p><p id=\"b326\"><b>Traps:</b> Anything that stops an error that has occurred from leading to harm. For example, having two surgeons operating at the same time or equipment redundancy</p><p id=\"b328\"><b>Mitigations:</b> Anything that stabilises a critical situation, reduces the impact of harm, and buys you more time to fix a safety-critical problem. Once harm has occurred, it’s all about damage control, buying time and stabilising the situation.</p><figure class=\"fig bowtie bowtie-b\" id=\"b331\"><div class=\"fig-in\"><img src=\"img/image9.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: bow-tie - threats lead to an error, which leads to harms; prevention before, recovery after.\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"bt-bar bt-barr\"><span>Barriers</span></span><span class=\"bt-bar bt-trap\"><span>Traps</span></span><span class=\"bt-bar bt-miti\"><span>Mitiagations</span></span></div></figure><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b332\">Reference: <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\"><b>Avoid, Trap, and Mitigate: Development of an Evidence-Based Crisis Management Framework in Surgery</b></a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\">. Journal of the American College of Surgeons 233(4</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\">):p</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\"> 536e1, October 2021. </a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\">Gogalniceanu</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.ovid.com/jnls/journalacs/abstract/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010~avoid-trap-and-mitigate-development-of-an-evidence-based?redirectionsource=fulltextview\"> et al</a></p>"}, {"id": "human-factors", "label": "Human factors", "group": "foundations", "title": "8. Human Factors", "html": "<h3 id=\"b336\">What are Human factors?</h3><p id=\"b337\">Human factors (HF) science is all about optimising human performance to undertake complex tasks in fragile environments.</p><p id=\"b339\">Put another way:</p><p id=\"b340\">Make the <b>best use </b>of all the <b>available resources </b>to obtain the <b>optimum outcome</b></p><p class=\"pull\" id=\"b342\"><i>“Human Factors: doing the best that you can with what you have to get what you want”</i></p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b343\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image10.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: a team caring for a patient in a hospital bed.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b346\">Human factors science acknowledges three facts: </h3><p id=\"b347\">1. The world is not perfect.</p><p id=\"b348\">2. Error is part of the human condition.</p><p id=\"b349\">3. If you do enough high-volume, high-risk work, harm can occur.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b351\"><b>The goal of human factors is to prevent errors and harm at all costs, but also to prepare to respond and recover should harm occur.</b></p><h3 id=\"b353\">Solution:</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b354\">We can all learn the skills, attitudes and behaviours that can help us better prepare, prevent, respond and recover from harm</li><li id=\"b355\">Our goal is to avoid harm, and when harm happens, to reduce its impact</li></ul><h3 id=\"b357\">Human Factors values: </h3><p class=\"pull\" id=\"b358\">Practical, Pragmatic, Proactive</p><h2 id=\"b362\">How do we think about Human Factors?</h2><p id=\"b364\">Human factors are based on three pillars, each consisting of 3 domains.</p><p id=\"b365\">Pillar 1: <b>Technical skills</b>:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b366\">Specialist subject-matter knowledge</li><li id=\"b367\">Technical skills and experience</li><li id=\"b368\">Technology: tools and equipment</li></ul><p id=\"b370\">Pillar 2: <b>Cognitive skills</b>:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b371\">Workload management</li><li id=\"b372\">Situational awareness</li><li id=\"b373\">Problem solving</li></ul><p id=\"b375\">Pillar 3: <b>Behavioural skills</b>:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b376\">Leadership &amp; teamwork</li><li id=\"b377\">Communication</li><li id=\"b378\">Professional standards</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b379\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image11.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the three pillars of human factors - technical, cognitive and behavioural skills.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b382\">Human Factors Model</h3><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b384\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image12.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the human factors model - three pillars of three domains each.\" width=\"1273\" height=\"737\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b385\">These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:</h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b386\">The <b>System</b> (environment) we work in</li><li id=\"b387\">The <b>Condition</b> of the human operators</li><li id=\"b388\"><b>Culture</b>: values, attitudes and habits we acquire from our environments</li></ol><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b390\"><b>When performance is lacking in one of these 9 domains, the remaining 8 can compensate. </b></p><p id=\"b392\">For example, if you lack knowledge or expertise in a particular area of work (technical skill), a good leader would have the situational awareness (cognitive skill) to realise this and leverage the experience of other team members through effective teamwork (behavioural skill) to make sure you are supported and the quality of the work is not affected.</p>"}, {"id": "technology", "label": "Technology", "group": "technical", "title": "9. Technical Skills", "html": "<h2 id=\"b400\">Technology</h2><p id=\"b401\">Technology refers to the <b>tools, equipment and innovations</b> that extend human capability and address human needs.</p><p id=\"b402\">However, technology is only as good as its use, design and integration.</p><h3 id=\"b404\">Technology can create complexity. </h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b405\">In human factors, simple works best.</li><li id=\"b406\">When new or complex technology is introduced, humans are more likely to make a mistake.</li><li id=\"b407\">This is why performance has a learning curve – until we build experience, we are more vulnerable to making a mistake.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b409\">Socio-Technical Interface</h3><p id=\"b410\">The interaction between a human and a machine is known as the <b>socio-technical interface</b>.</p><p id=\"b411\">Errors are likely to occur at this interface.</p><p id=\"b412\">Barriers, traps, and mitigations need to be inserted to address these risks.</p><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b414\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How to fix technology risk</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b415\">Safety by design - make it easy for people to do things right and hard to do things wrong.</li><li id=\"b417\">Training - mentorship, simulation and high case volume before independent use.</li><li id=\"b419\">Responsible use - an ethical duty to use the tool for what it is for, and to stop when it is the wrong tool.</li><li id=\"b421\">Governance and legislation - the rules that hold when enthusiasm runs ahead of evidence.</li></ul></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b423\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Shape-coded cockpit controls (Aviation · Second World War)</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b424\">As aircraft grew more complex, crews began retracting the landing gear on the runway instead of the flaps. The fix was not more training or more discipline: the landing gear lever was given a small wheel on the end and the flaps lever a flat paddle. You could tell the controls apart by hand, in the dark, under stress. This is safety by design in one object - and why theatre kit, connectors, and infusion lines are increasingly shaped so they physically cannot be misconnected.</p></div></aside><h3 id=\"b426\">SHELL Model</h3><p id=\"b427\">The SHELL Model illustrates how the socio-technical interface operates.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b428\"><b>S</b> - Software - The intangibles: policies, protocols, procedures, the electronic record, the way the list is written.</li><li id=\"b429\"><b>H</b> - Hardware - Equipment, instruments, monitors, pumps, buildings, furniture.</li><li id=\"b430\"><b>E</b> - Environment - Light, noise, heat, space, time of day, interruption load.</li><li id=\"b431\"><b>L</b> - Liveware (you) - The person at the centre: their skill, condition, attention and limits.</li><li id=\"b432\"><b>L</b> - Liveware (others) - Everyone else - the team, other specialties, the patient and their family.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b433\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image13.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the SHELL model of the socio-technical interface.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b437\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">\"Humans are at the same time the strongest and the weakest link in any system.\"</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b438\">Technology always requires human supervision.</p><p id=\"b439\">Technology also compensates for human limitations.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "knowledge", "label": "Knowledge, skill and experience", "group": "technical", "title": "Knowledge · Skill · Experience", "html": "<p class=\"lead\" id=\"b444\">Without skill, knowledge and experience, no amount of human factors training can create safe and highly performant outcomes</p><h3 id=\"b446\">Knowledge</h3><p id=\"b447\">Knowledge requires study. You need to know the facts, understand how they relate, and build a mental model of how they work in practice. You also need to understand how they fail, so that you can prevent, detect and fix errors once they occur.</p><p id=\"b449\">You can think of knowledge in 3 categories:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b450\">Form: how things are made or put together</li><li id=\"b451\">Function: how they work</li><li id=\"b452\">Failure: how they break down</li></ul><h3 id=\"b455\">Skill and Experience</h3><p id=\"b456\">It is not enough to know. You also have to be able to do. You have to be a practitioner. This requires real-world experience and case volume.</p><p id=\"b457\">Often learning occurs by doing.</p><p id=\"b458\">Skills need practice, rehearsal, and refinement.</p><p id=\"b459\">Failure is part of learning, but it must occur in controlled environments, with adequate supervision, mentorship and feedback.</p><p id=\"b460\">This makes <b>simulation</b> an essential tool – it allows you to fail safely and learn from your mistakes without putting anyone at risk or in danger.</p><p id=\"b461\">The process of learning from failures is called “failing well” or “failing forward”</p><h3 id=\"b463\">Deliberate Practice</h3><p id=\"b464\">Deliberate practice improves performance through repetition, feedback, and iterative refinement.</p><p id=\"b465\">Practice does not make perfect performance. Perfect practice makes perfect performance.</p><p id=\"b466\">This is why personal discipline, determination and mentorship are essential.</p><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b468\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">for Subject Matter Expertise</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b469\">Know first principles – the universal ‘rules of the road’</li><li id=\"b470\">Have frameworks and mental models</li><li id=\"b471\">Do the basic things exceedingly well.</li><li id=\"b472\">Practice, have mentorship, ask for feedback.</li></ol></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b474\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The difference between amateurs and professionals is:</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b475\">Attention to detail</li><li id=\"b476\">Ownership of the problem, solution, and outcome</li><li id=\"b477\">Follow-up and continuous learning</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"id": "workload", "label": "Workload management", "group": "cognitive", "title": "11. Cognitive Skills", "html": "<h2 id=\"b483\">Workload management</h2><h3 id=\"b484\">Principles: </h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b485\">You can think or you can execute - you can’t do both well at the same time.</li><li id=\"b486\">The higher the workload, the lower your situational awareness.</li><li id=\"b487\">You can do anything, but not everything</li></ol><h3 id=\"b489\">What makes work ‘hard’?</h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b490\">Disruptions</li><li id=\"b491\">Thinking and doing at the same time</li><li id=\"b492\">New or complex tasks</li><li id=\"b493\">Conflict or uncertainty</li><li id=\"b494\">Fear</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b496\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image14.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: workload management - a nurse surrounded by disruptions, fear, new tasks and uncertainty.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b499\">How do you manage high workloads?</h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b500\">Offload tasks &amp; delegate appropriately</li><li id=\"b501\">Make a “To do” List</li><li id=\"b502\">Prioritise items: differentiate between urgent and important tasks</li><li id=\"b503\">Use the Eisenhower Matrix (below) to address tasks</li></ol><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b505\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Eisenhower prioritisation matrix</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b506\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image15.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the Eisenhower decision matrix - do, decide, delegate, delete.\" width=\"1038\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b509\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Sterile cockpit</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b510\">During phases of work that are critical or high risk, avoid all non-essential conversation and non-task-related activities or noise</li><li id=\"b511\">Operators should only have safety-critical or mission-relevant discussions</li><li id=\"b512\">An enforced distraction-free environment is known as a ‘sterile cockpit’</li><li id=\"b513\">These allow focus and concentration on high-risk tasks</li><li id=\"b514\">Examples include anaesthetic induction, the instrument or swab count in the operating room, drawing up opioid drugs, patient handover, difficult parts of an operation, working in dangerous circumstances.</li><li id=\"b515\">Say it out loud so the rule is shared, not in private: <i>“Can we have a sterile cockpit until we've finished the count, please. Thank you!”</i></li></ul><p id=\"b517\"><b><i>Remember:</i></b> The Sterile Cockpit tool does not prevent people from speaking up about safety. Its precisely there so people can flag safety concerns without being drowned out by the background noise.</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b519\"><div class=\"co-tag\">Note</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The ‘sterile cockpit’ principles is borrowed from commercial aviation, where the rule below 10,000 feet is that nothing is discussed except flying the aircraft.</i></h3><div class=\"co-body\"></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b521\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Dual operators</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b522\">When doing high-risk / high-intensity work split the roles: one to do, one to think (monitor)</li><li id=\"b523\">The acting operator drives the task; the monitoring operator watches the whole picture, the clock and the patient.</li><li id=\"b524\">The monitor must be free of hands-on work, otherwise they are not monitoring properly.</li><li id=\"b525\">Plan to swap roles deliberately before the acting operator saturates their cognitive capacity</li></ul></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b527\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Work to standard</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b528\">Create simple routines and standardised flows, so that routine cases require no active effort or repetition (for example, use templates whenever possible)</li><li id=\"b529\">Write the routine down as a checklist to execute a standard operating procedure (SOP).</li><li id=\"b530\">Keep it short enough to be used.</li><li id=\"b531\">Standardise the layout as well as the steps - same trolley, same order, same place, every time.</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"id": "awareness", "label": "Situational awareness", "group": "cognitive", "title": "Situational awareness", "html": "<h3 id=\"b536\">Cognitive Capacity</h3><p id=\"b537\">Cognitive capacity is your mental ‘bandwidth’</p><p id=\"b538\">If you want to think better, reduce workload and increase situational awareness</p><h3 id=\"b540\">Situational awareness</h3><p id=\"b541\">Situational awareness (SA) is your ability to notice, understand and think ahead</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b543\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image16.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: situational awareness - notice, understand, think ahead.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"636\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b546\">Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)</h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b547\"><b>Notice → What?</b> What just happened? What is actually in front of me?</li><li id=\"b548\"><b>Understand → So what?</b> What does that mean for this patient, this list, this team?</li><li id=\"b549\"><b>Think ahead → Now what?</b> Where is this going, and what will I need in five minutes, 5 hours, or 5 days from now?</li></ol><h3 id=\"b551\">What decays situational awareness?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b552\">Physical factors - fatigue, hunger, dehydration</li><li id=\"b553\">Cognitive factors - high workload, distractions</li><li id=\"b554\">Emotional factors - stress, fear, anger</li></ul><p id=\"b555\">Losing awareness means losing the ability to monitor, to process, or to predict - and you generally lose them in that order without noticing any of it.</p><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b557\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The Capacity Bucket</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b558\">The capacity bucket is the simplest way to understand situational awareness:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b559\">Everyone has a capacity bucket. The more things you throw in the bucket, the less space is left for situational awareness. This includes noise, fatigue, the difficult conversation from an hour ago.</li><li id=\"b560\">Situational awareness represents whatever space is left.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b561\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image17.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the capacity bucket - workload fills cognitive capacity, leaving situational awareness above it.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"674\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b562\">Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kaafarani H. Am I safe? Surgery. 2022;171(2):556-7. doi:10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-case\" id=\"b565\"><div class=\"co-tag\">CASE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Eastern Air Lines Flight 401 (Florida Everglades · 29 December 1972)</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b566\">A Lockheed TriStar approaching Miami showed an unlit landing gear indicator. All three flight crew turned their attention to a two-dollar light bulb. Somewhere in that discussion, the autopilot disconnected, and the aircraft entered a gentle descent nobody was watching. It flew into the Everglades. 101 people died; 75 survived. The gear was down the whole time. The classic fixation accident: a whole crew, fully awake, entirely competent, and with a perfectly functional aircraft – BUT with 100% of their attention on 1% of the problem.</p></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b568\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The 2-challenge rule</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b569\">Assertiveness tools build the situational awareness of someone in a higher hierarchical role who may be cognitively disoriented. They allow a stepwise escalation in assertiveness.</p><p id=\"b570\">They empower individuals to speak up and ultimately take action when someone has lost situational awareness.</p><p id=\"b572\">This can occur in three stages:</p><p id=\"b573\">1. <b>Prompt</b> - nudge or draw attention to something that is not right. Most of the time, people will respond to a prompt and be grateful for it. “By the way, have you noticed that blood vessel?” It’s soft and curious, and it is easy to accept.</p><p id=\"b575\">2. <b>Direct</b> - if you get no response from the prompt, say what needs to be done in a simple and direct way: “Stop cutting there.” This is an unambiguous instruction that names the action.</p><p id=\"b577\">3. <b>Intervene</b> - if someone has not responded to two challenges of increasing assertiveness, it means they have completely lost situational awareness and will not respond to a third; it is time to act.  Gently take over and carry out the intended action yourself. In this situation, you have given fair warning, and you are obliged to escalate. The second unanswered challenge is the trigger.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b580\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image18.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the 2-challenge rule - prompt, direct, intervene.\" width=\"1274\" height=\"463\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b582\">This approach works only in an organisation that has adopted this policy and fosters a culture acceptable to the entire workforce.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "decisions", "label": "Decision making and problem solving", "group": "cognitive", "title": "Decision making and problem solving", "html": "<h3 id=\"b587\">How do you make good decisions?</h3><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b588\"><b>Cognitive Space.</b> First, create the mental bandwidth to make good decisions. This is called cognitive capacity. To build it,  reduce workload, increase situational awareness and avoid distractions.</li><li id=\"b590\"><b>Emergency Protocols / Checklists.</b> Check whether protocols are in place to help you fix the problem you face. Is there an emergency checklist or Emergency Operating Procedure available? If yes, use it. It’s there for a reason. No protocol? You will have to problem-solve.</li><li id=\"b592\"><b>Analytic problem-solving:</b> work out the solution from scratch</li></ol><h3 id=\"b594\">How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method </h3><p id=\"b596\">The T-DODAR tool allows you to think logically through a problem. Each letter stands for a step in the process.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b598\"><b>T</b> - Time - How long have I got to solve the problem? This answer sets every other answer. Do I need to rush to a quick solution, or can I take my time?</li><li id=\"b599\"><b>D</b> - Diagnosis - What is actually happening? Gather opinions from the whole team, not just the loudest voice. How bad is it? How might things evolve?</li><li id=\"b600\"><b>O</b> - Options - What could we do? An option is not a decision. Make a list of all the possible solutions. For each option, list <b>b</b>enefits and <b>r</b>isks. List <b>a</b>lternatives, including the option of doing <b>n</b>othing (BRAN model). Don’t forget to include options you don’t like or that are ‘out of the box’.</li><li id=\"b601\"><b>D</b> - Decide – Analyse all options and choose the single best one. Only one! Name the choice so everyone hears the same plan.</li><li id=\"b602\"><b>A</b> - Assign task and Act – Decide who does what, and by when. Allocate tasks by name.</li><li id=\"b603\"><b>R</b> - Review - Measure the outcome of your intervention. Use metrics and be specific. Did it work? If not, run the cycle again. Don’t skip this step! Remember it’s a cycle</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b604\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image19.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the T-DODAR cycle.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b607\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">T-DODAR Analytic Problem Solving</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b608\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image20.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: T-DODAR steps - time, diagnosis, options, decide, assign and act, review - repeated if it did not work.\" width=\"1273\" height=\"364\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure></div></aside><h3 id=\"b610\">How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?</h3><p id=\"b611\"><b>1. Use First Principles. </b>If you can’t work out how to fix a problem, think of the first principles. One such example: <i>“Never walk away from a sick patient”.</i> These are ‘rules of the road’ that guide actions but can lead to poor outcomes if applied incorrectly.</p><p id=\"b612\">2. Innovate: This is a trial-and-error approach. You see a problem, develop an idea, and create a prototype solution. Then test an trial that solution in a controlled and safe environment. You test to make it fail; you learn from that failure, then improve the prototype and repeat the cycle until you have a product that can be adopted. This approach has safety limitations in healthcare and should be used only within appropriate ethical, innovation, and governance frameworks.</p><h4 id=\"b613\">Innovation Cycle</h4><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b614\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image21.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the innovation cycle - problem, idea, prototype, test, fail and learn, adapt or adopt.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b616\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Innovative Problem Solving</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"cycle\" id=\"b617\"><li>Problem</li><li>Idea</li><li>Prototype</li><li>Test &amp; Trial</li><li>Fail &amp; Learn</li><li>Adapt / Adopt</li></ol></div></aside><h3 id=\"b619\">Why do smart people make bad decisions?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b620\"><b>A</b>ttitudes: Hazardous attitudes - machismo, invincibility, anti-authority, impulsivity, resignation.</li><li id=\"b621\"><b>B</b>iases:  shortcuts our brain uses to make quick decisions; they aren’t always right and can get us into trouble.  Examples: recency bias (being swayed by the last case you saw), optimism bias (it will be fine), fixation bias (focusing on one issue, whilst ignoring everything else).</li><li id=\"b622\"><b>C</b>ondition: Operator deconditioning - stress, fear, fatigue, overstimulation.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b624\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image22.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: five common cognitive biases in healthcare.\" width=\"1222\" height=\"1287\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b626\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">A, B, C</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b627\">Attitudes, biases, and conditions are not knowledge deficits, which is why more teaching does not fix them.</p><p id=\"b628\">Address them by leading by example, providing strong role models, challenging them, and looking after the operator.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "leadership", "label": "Leadership, followership and teamwork", "group": "behavioural", "title": "12. Behavioural", "html": "<h2 id=\"b632\">Leadership, followership and teamwork</h2><h3 id=\"b634\">What is leadership?</h3><blockquote class=\"quote\" id=\"b635\"><p>\"Leadership is the ability to drive positive change for those we serve.\"</p></blockquote><p class=\"cite\" id=\"b636\"><i>P Gogalniceanu</i></p><blockquote class=\"quote\" id=\"b638\"><p>\"Management is about doing things right; leadership is about doing the right things.\"</p></blockquote><p class=\"cite\" id=\"b639\">Peter Drucker</p><p class=\"pull\" id=\"b640\"><i>“Leaders are changemakers. They take people safely from A to B.”</i></p><figure class=\"fig portrait\" id=\"b641\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image23.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: What is leadership? A leader guiding people along a path from A to B.\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1536\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b642\">Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32–42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002.</p><h3 id=\"b645\">Teamwork &amp; Followership</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b646\">Leadership, followership and teamwork are three roles that we all need to play.</li><li id=\"b647\">You should be able to move between roles independent of your experience, seniority or position in the organisation.</li><li id=\"b648\">You can’t be a good leader if you can’t follow or work as part of a team</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b650\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image24.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: teamwork and followership - lead, follow, work together.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b653\">What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?</h3><p id=\"b654\"><b>Hierarchy</b> is the role we play in an organisation. It is set and non-negotiable.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b655\">It’s a normal social structure that helps us operate efficiently.</li><li id=\"b656\">It gives us the legitimacy to do our jobs.</li><li id=\"b657\">Without hierarchy, there is chaos.</li></ul><p id=\"b659\"><b>Authority</b> is how we choose to use the power that our hierarchical position gives us.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b660\">If we choose to be authoritative and formal, then we use a steep authority gradient</li><li id=\"b661\">If we are approachable and informal, we are applying a shallow authority gradient</li><li id=\"b662\">There is no such thing as a flat authority gradient, as we all operate within a hierarchical structure – we all report to someone and receive reports from others.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b663\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image25.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: hierarchy and authority - a steep and a shallow authority gradient.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b664\">Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32–42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002.</p><h3 id=\"b666\">How do we get the right authority gradient?</h3><p id=\"b667\">In most situations, we should use a shallow authority gradient - that is, we should not rely on positional power to get things done. Instead, we should use clear explanations, good morale and motivation to encourage people to follow our plans or instructions.</p><p id=\"b668\">This means including all team members in discussions, involving them in decisions, asking for their opinions, listening to their views, and working collaboratively to achieve the best possible outcome. Compromise is a key part of this, unless you are compromising on safety, in which case you need to draw a clear line.</p><p id=\"b669\">The authority gradient can steepen when safety is at risk, incivility occurs, norms are deliberately broken, or disrespectful behaviour is seen. In such cases, we can be more directive or instructional.</p><p id=\"b670\">Getting the authority gradient wrong damages trust, respect and relationships, and ultimately compromises teamwork, followership and leadership.</p><p id=\"b671\">Remember: adjust your authority gradient to the task's complexity and risk, as well as to the team you are leading.</p><p id=\"b672\">Be an adaptive leader.</p><h3 id=\"b673\">What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b674\"><b>Competence:</b> can do the job and deliver results – does the basic things exceedingly well.</li><li id=\"b675\"><b>Character</b>: does what is right even when it’s hard, and no one is looking (integrity).</li><li id=\"b676\"><b>Courage:</b> acts responsibly in the face of adversity</li><li id=\"b677\"><b>Communication:</b> clear, simple, consistent.</li><li id=\"b678\"><b>Curiosity:</b> is willing to learn and try new things.</li><li id=\"b679\"><b>Charisma:</b> motivates people to follow them.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b681\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How to improve your leadership game</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b682\">Safety first: you are the last line of defence between harm and your people</li><li id=\"b683\">Lead by example</li><li id=\"b684\">Set the tone for asking question, challenging decisions and speaking up</li><li id=\"b685\">Keep your word and promises</li><li id=\"b686\">Be kind &amp; fair</li><li id=\"b687\">Take responsibility / Take things seriously</li><li id=\"b688\">Give simple and clear directions</li><li id=\"b689\">Keep a good lookout → check &amp; anticipate</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"id": "communication", "label": "Communication", "group": "behavioural", "title": "Communication", "html": "<h3 id=\"b694\">What is Communication?</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b695\">Communication is the process of transmitting and receiving information: intentions, instructions, feelings or facts.</li><li id=\"b696\">It requires: 1) a sender or transmitter; 2) a receiver; and 3) a message that travels between the two through 4) a medium or channel.</li><li id=\"b697\">That medium can be our voice, hand signals, a text message, email or a whistle - anything that gets the message across.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b698\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image26.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: what is communication - a sender, a receiver, a message and a channel.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h3 id=\"b700\">What is noise?</h3><p id=\"b701\">Every medium is vulnerable to noise: anything that distorts, blocks or changes the message, such as background interference, stress, fatigue, distraction, assumptions or jargon.</p><h3 id=\"b703\">Good and Bad Communication</h3><p id=\"b704\">A large proportion of avoidable medical errors, patient harm and lawsuits (!) are caused by poor communication.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b705\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image27.webp\" alt=\"Cartoon: a sinking sailor calls &quot;We are sinking!&quot; and the radio operator replies &quot;What are you thinking about?&quot;\" width=\"1272\" height=\"747\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b706\">Communication is not what you say, but what the other person understands.</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b707\">The quality of our communication depends on the quality of the transmission, reception, message and medium.</li><li id=\"b708\">Just because someone hears something, it does not mean they will process or understand the message.</li><li id=\"b709\">People are primed to receive information by how we make them feel, as well as by our non-verbal communication.</li><li id=\"b710\">Noisy environments or distracting circumstances reduce our ability to transmit, receive and process information.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b712\">Think well, Speak well, Write well</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b713\">When you think, speak or write follow the ABC rule: <b>A</b>ccuracy, <b>B</b>revity, <b>C</b>larity</li><li id=\"b714\">Have clarity of thought before you decide to communicate. What is the one point you are trying to get across?</li><li id=\"b715\">Think of the speed, clarity and tone of your voice.</li><li id=\"b716\">Speak slowly, calmly and confidently.</li><li id=\"b717\">Never write an email when angry or upset.</li><li id=\"b718\">Remember the rules of good writing: short sentences, short paragraphs</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b720\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How do you improve communication?</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b721\">Listen first and most; speak second and least.</li><li id=\"b722\">Do not interrupt.</li><li id=\"b723\">Use people's names.</li><li id=\"b724\">Keep eye contact; speak face to face.</li><li id=\"b725\">Meet in person or call rather use email in any situation where tone is important or people might misunderstand intention.</li><li id=\"b726\">Use open body language.</li></ol></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b728\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Crisis communication or briefing</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b729\">Use the NITS framework to communicate in a crisis:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b730\"><b>N</b>ature of the problem</li><li id=\"b731\"><b>I</b>ntentions: what you are going to do about it?</li><li id=\"b732\"><b>T</b>imeline</li><li id=\"b733\"><b>S</b>pecial instructions: what you would like others to do now</li></ul></div></aside><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b735\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Closed Loop Communication</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><h4 id=\"b736\">What is closed-loop communication?</h4><p id=\"b737\">Closed-loop communication is the discipline of confirming receipt and understanding of a message by readback and verification, so that no instruction or action is misunderstood.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b739\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image28.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: closed-loop communication - instruction, readback, verification.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h4 id=\"b740\">How does it work?</h4><p id=\"b741\">Closed-loop communication: the receiver confirms what they heard (readback), and the sender checks that it was understood correctly (hearback).</p><h4 id=\"b743\">Example:</h4><p id=\"b744\">During a ward round, the consultant says: \"Can you give 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones, please.\"</p><p id=\"b745\">The nurse replies: \"Just to confirm - 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones in bed 4?\" (<b>readback</b>)</p><p id=\"b746\">The consultant responds: \"That's correct.\" (<b>hearback</b>)</p><p id=\"b747\">The nurse administers the drug and later reports back: \"Paracetamol 1 gram IV given at 14:00, Mrs Jones in bed 4.\" (<b>confirmation</b> that the action is complete)</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "professional", "label": "Professional standards", "group": "behavioural", "title": "Professional standards", "html": "<h3 id=\"b751\"><i>What are professional standards?</i></h3><p id=\"b752\">The behavioural hallmarks of what good looks like.</p><blockquote class=\"quote\" id=\"b754\"><p><b><i>“It must be done right!”</i></b></p></blockquote><p class=\"cite\" id=\"b755\">Robert Linton</p><p id=\"b756\">Professionalism is about how you respect yourself, others and your work.</p><p id=\"b758\">As a professional, you are committed to maintaining the high standard.</p><aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b760\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Hallmarks of professional work</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b761\">Do it properly (do it right)</li><li id=\"b762\">Do it all (do it in the right amount)</li><li id=\"b763\">Do it on time</li><li id=\"b764\">Do it consistently (do it over time)</li></ul></div></aside><h3 id=\"b766\">What does professionalism look like?</h3><h4 id=\"b767\">The five Cs</h4><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b768\"><b>C</b>ompetence - The technical ability to do the work to standard.</li><li id=\"b769\"><b>C</b>ondition - Turning up fit to do the job.</li><li id=\"b770\"><b>C</b>ivility - How you treat people while you do it. Rudeness measurably degrades team performance.</li><li id=\"b771\"><b>C</b>onviction - Doing it the same way even when it is inconvenient, unobserved or unrewarded.</li><li id=\"b772\"><b>C</b>omportment – how you choose to carry yourself and behave towards others.</li></ul><aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b774\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Why civility sits in a safety document</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b775\">Incivility damages psychological safety and causes emotional trauma,</p><p id=\"b776\">A team that has been spoken to badly asks fewer questions, offers fewer challenges and reports fewer concerns.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "summary", "label": "Summary", "group": "reference", "title": "Summary of Human Factors", "html": "<h3 id=\"b781\">Summary</h3><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b782\">‘Human factors’ is the science of human performance.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b783\">It is understanding how we function and fail, and doing our best to avoid, trap, and mitigate error.</p><p id=\"b784\">It is about making the most of what we have to get what we need.</p><p id=\"b786\">Human performance is affected by five factors:</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b787\" class=\"l1\">Technical and non-technical (human factors) skills.</li><li id=\"b788\" class=\"l1\">Operator condition: physical, cognitive and emotional.</li><li id=\"b789\" class=\"l1\">Culture (personal and organisational): values, attitudes, habits and identities.</li><li id=\"b790\" class=\"l1\">Systems: the people, places, tools and rules available to do work.</li><li id=\"b791\" class=\"l1\">Task: complexity, size and urgency</li></ol><p id=\"b793\">There are three parts to human factors:</p><h3 id=\"b794\">Technical considerations</h3><p id=\"b795\">Technology</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b796\">Tools extend the ability to do work.</li><li id=\"b797\">Technology also adds complexity and increases the risk of errors occurring</li><li id=\"b798\">Safety by design and ergonomics improve how humans interact with machines.</li></ul><p id=\"b800\">Technical skills refer to an individual's knowledge, experience, and physical ability to do work.</p><h3 id=\"b802\">2. Cognitive skills</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b803\"><b>Workload management</b>: Offload tasks appropriately. Apply the sterile cockpit and use dual operators.</li><li id=\"b805\"><b>Situational awareness</b>: Notice, understand, think ahead (NUTa). Protect the cognitive capacity.</li><li id=\"b807\"><b>Problem solving</b>: Create Cognitive Capacity → EOP → T-DODAR → first principles.</li></ul><h3 id=\"b809\">3. Behavioural skills</h3><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b810\"><b>Leadership </b>is the ability to drive positive change for those we serve;</li></ul><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b811\">Lead by example</li><li id=\"b812\">Take responsibility.</li><li id=\"b813\">Be humbly confident.</li><li id=\"b814\">You can’t be a leader if you can’t work as a team member and follow others (even if they have a less senior hierarchical role than you).</li></ol><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b816\"><b>Communication</b>: Keep things short, clear and specific. Listen first, speak second. Remember: body language, tone and eye contact.</li><li id=\"b818\"><b>Professional standards</b>: Maintain the high standard and demand it of yourself and others.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "glossary", "label": "Glossary", "group": "reference", "title": "Glossary", "html": "<dl class=\"glossary\"><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b840\"><dt><b>Authority</dt><dd></b>How we choose to use the power that our hierarchical position gives us.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b841\"><dt><b>Barriers</dt><dd></b>Anything that stops or avoids a threat from becoming an error.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b842\"><dt><b>Biases</dt><dd></b>Shortcuts our brain uses to make quick decisions; they aren’t always right and can get us into trouble.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b843\"><dt><b>Bow-tie analysis</dt><dd></b>A simple way of looking at the cause and effect of any hazard or failure - what leads into an error on one side, and what is the impact (harms) on the other.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b844\"><dt><b>Closed-loop communication</dt><dd></b>The discipline of confirming receipt and understanding of a message by readback and verification, so that no instruction or action is misunderstood.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b845\"><dt><b>Cognitive capacity</dt><dd></b>Your mental “bandwidth”.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b846\"><dt><b>Communication</dt><dd></b>The process of transmitting and receiving information: intentions, instructions, feelings or facts.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b847\"><dt><b>Crises</dt><dd></b>Situations where harms have not been correctly mitigated, leading to novel, uncertain and time-critical situations with multiple competing priorities.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b848\"><dt><b>Culture</dt><dd></b>The values, attitudes and habits that shape how we feel, think and behave.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b849\"><dt><b>Deliberate practice</dt><dd></b>Improves performance through repetition, feedback, and iterative refinement.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b850\"><dt><b>Errors</dt><dd></b>Unintended outcomes - also known as system “failures”. Errors themselves don’t necessarily lead to harm.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b851\"><dt><b>Harm</dt><dd></b>Errors that have negatively impacted someone or something, for example a patient, a member of staff or the organisation.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b852\"><dt><b>Health system</dt><dd></b>Consists of four elements: people, places, tools and rules.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b853\"><dt><b>Hierarchy</dt><dd></b>The role we play in an organisation. It is set and non-negotiable.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b854\"><dt><b>Human factors</dt><dd></b>The science of optimising human performance to undertake complex tasks in fragile environments.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b855\"><dt><b>Just culture</dt><dd></b>Accountability is attributed to individuals fairly and proportionally, with support, training or disciplinary action according to the nature of the error.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b856\"><dt><b>Leadership</dt><dd></b>The ability to drive positive change for those we serve.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b857\"><dt><b>Mitigations</dt><dd></b>Anything that stabilises a critical situation, reduces the impact of harm, and buys more time to fix a safety-critical problem.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b858\"><dt><b>Noise</dt><dd></b>Anything that distorts, blocks or changes the message.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b859\"><dt><b>Operator condition</dt><dd></b>Physical, cognitive and emotional status that limits our ability to perform tasks.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b860\"><dt><b>Professional standards</dt><dd></b>The behavioural hallmarks of what good looks like.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b861\"><dt><b>Safety by design</dt><dd></b>Make it easy for people to do things right and hard to do things wrong.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b862\"><dt><b>Situational awareness</dt><dd></b>The ability to notice, understand and think ahead.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b863\"><dt><b>Socio-technical interface</dt><dd></b>The interaction between a human and a machine.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b864\"><dt><b>Startle</dt><dd></b>A reaction where your emotions take over your thinking. You stop being rational and logical.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b865\"><dt><b>Sterile cockpit</dt><dd></b>An enforced distraction-free environment during critical or high-risk phases of work.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b866\"><dt><b>Technical skills</dt><dd></b>An individual’s knowledge, experience, and physical ability to do work.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b867\"><dt><b>Technology</dt><dd></b>The tools, equipment and innovations that extend human capability and address human needs.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b868\"><dt><b>Threats</dt><dd></b>Any risk factors that make errors more likely; conditions that push towards failure.</dd></div><div class=\"gl-row\" id=\"b869\"><dt><b>Traps</dt><dd></b>Anything that stops an error that has occurred from leading to harm.</dd></div></dl>"}, {"id": "references", "label": "References and acknowledgement", "group": "reference", "title": "References and acknowledgement", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-note\" id=\"b823\"><div class=\"co-tag\">NOTE</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Original content</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b824\">All original content in this guide is derived from the source below and should not be reproduced or shared without the author's permission.</p><blockquote class=\"quote\" id=\"b825\"><p>\"High Performance in Healthcare - Optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery.\"</p></blockquote><p class=\"cite\" id=\"b826\"><i>- P Gogalniceanu, King's College London, 2019</i></p><p id=\"b827\">Delivered as Principles of Human Factors by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu (2026, all rights reserved)</p></div></aside><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b829\">1. Gogalniceanu P. High performance in healthcare: optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery [teaching material]. London: King’s College London; 2019.</p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b830\">2. Wiegmann DA, Shappell SA. A human error analysis of commercial aviation accidents using the Human Factors Analysis and Classification System (HFACS). Washington (DC): Federal Aviation Administration, Office of Aviation Medicine; 2001. Report No.: DOT/FAA/AM-01/3. Available from: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf</a></p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b831\">3. Smits M, Zegers M, Groenewegen PP, Timmermans DRM, Zwaan L, van der Wal G, et al. Exploring the causes of adverse events in hospitals and potential prevention strategies. Qual Saf Health Care. 2010;19(5):e5. doi:10.1136/qshc.2008.030726. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1136/qshc.2008.030726\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.1136/qshc.2008.030726</a></p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b832\">4. Gogalniceanu P, Karydis N, Kessaris N, Olsburgh J, Calder F, Sevdalis N, et al. How crises work: a model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. Int J Surg. 2022;104:106711. doi:10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711</a></p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b833\">5. Gogalniceanu P, Karydis N, Loukopoulos I, Kessaris N, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Avoid, trap, and mitigate: development of an evidence-based crisis management framework in surgery. J Am Coll Surg. 2021;233(4):526-536.e1. doi:10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010</a></p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b834\">6. Gogalniceanu P, Kaafarani H. Am I safe? Surgery. 2022;171(2):556-557. doi:10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007</a></p><p class=\"refitem\" id=\"b835\">7. Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32-42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002</a></p>"}], "tools": [{"page": "operator", "label": "Operator condition", "id": "b119", "title": "3 Principles for optimising operator condition", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b119\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">3 Principles for optimising operator condition</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b120\">You have to be ‘fit for the job’. Optimise yourself to optimise your outcomes. Think body, mind and emotions.</li><li id=\"b121\">Never do safety-critical work if you are acutely unwell or have not slept.</li><li id=\"b122\">Manage your energy: eat, sleep, hydrate, move, socialise.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "startle", "label": "Startle syndrome", "id": "b148", "title": "Startle-management toolkit", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b148\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Startle-management toolkit</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b149\">How do you manage your startle syndrome?</p><h4 id=\"b150\">Startle Tool 1: ‘Stop and Sit on your hands”</h4><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b151\">“Sit on your hands”. Fight the impulse to jump to solutions. The first instinct is rarely the right one.</li><li id=\"b152\">Stop - do nothing for a moment. Very few emergencies are made worse by three seconds of doing nothing.</li><li id=\"b153\">Distract the distraction: count to 30, take 10 deep breaths, make a hot drink, go for a walk, play with your watch.</li><li id=\"b154\">Stay calm and methodical: Re-orient using a structure you already know by heart.</li></ol><h4 id=\"b157\">Startle Tool 2: Have a mental model or cognitive re-orientation tool you can fall back on.</h4><p id=\"b159\">Some examples of mental models or tools to re-orient in a startled situation:</p><h4 id=\"b160\">ABCD (Medical)</h4><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b161\">Airway</li><li id=\"b162\">Breathing</li><li id=\"b163\">Circulation</li><li id=\"b164\">Disability</li></ul><h4 id=\"b166\">ABCD (Surgical)</h4><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b167\">Access</li><li id=\"b168\">Bleeding control</li><li id=\"b169\">Contamination/infection control</li><li id=\"b170\">Decide: damage control vs definitive care</li><li id=\"b171\">Exit (damage control) or Escalate (get help in definitive care)</li></ul><h4 id=\"b173\">PMC</h4><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b174\">Priority actions: critical tasks</li><li id=\"b175\">Move: move people, mobilise resources, modify environments</li><li id=\"b176\">Communicate</li></ul><h4 id=\"b178\">4Ps</h4><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b179\">Patient</li><li id=\"b180\">Place</li><li id=\"b181\">Procedure</li><li id=\"b182\">Personnel (staff)</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"page": "culture", "label": "Culture", "id": "b242", "title": "Just Culture", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b242\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Just Culture</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b243\">When an unintended outcome occurs, we need to understand why.</p><p id=\"b245\">This helps us learn and avoid it happening again. It’s about safety, not blame. The next person to be harmed could be you, me or someone we care about.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b247\">Justice arises from the fair attribution of responsibility rather than looking for someone to blame.</p><p class=\"lead\" id=\"b249\">It is important to first ask <b>“Why did this happen?”</b> rather than <b>“Who did it?”</b></p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b251\">Only then can we debrief fairly, transparently and openly in order to learn.</li><li id=\"b254\">A just culture does not mean we always find systems and circumstances to blame instead of holding individuals accountable.</li><li id=\"b255\">A just culture asks a prior question - was this a genuine error, a risky choice, or reckless behaviour? - and applies a different response to each.</li><li id=\"b256\">Staff will report threats and errors only when they know they will be treated fairly and trust a system that holds people accountable when it should.</li></ul><p id=\"b258\">In a just culture, accountability is attributed to individuals fairly and proportionally.</p><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b259\">In an innocent mistake, such as a system failure or misunderstanding, support is provided to the individual involved</li><li id=\"b260\">In an error of inexperience, lack of skill or knowledge, training or coaching is provided</li><li id=\"b261\">If the error occurred due to negligence, inappropriate risk-taking behaviour or wilful misdoing, appropriate disciplinary action may be considered.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "technology", "label": "Technology", "id": "b414", "title": "How to fix technology risk", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b414\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How to fix technology risk</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b415\">Safety by design - make it easy for people to do things right and hard to do things wrong.</li><li id=\"b417\">Training - mentorship, simulation and high case volume before independent use.</li><li id=\"b419\">Responsible use - an ethical duty to use the tool for what it is for, and to stop when it is the wrong tool.</li><li id=\"b421\">Governance and legislation - the rules that hold when enthusiasm runs ahead of evidence.</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"page": "knowledge", "label": "Knowledge, skill and experience", "id": "b468", "title": "for Subject Matter Expertise", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b468\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">for Subject Matter Expertise</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b469\">Know first principles – the universal ‘rules of the road’</li><li id=\"b470\">Have frameworks and mental models</li><li id=\"b471\">Do the basic things exceedingly well.</li><li id=\"b472\">Practice, have mentorship, ask for feedback.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "workload", "label": "Workload management", "id": "b505", "title": "Eisenhower prioritisation matrix", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b505\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Eisenhower prioritisation matrix</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b506\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image15.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the Eisenhower decision matrix - do, decide, delegate, delete.\" width=\"1038\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure></div></aside>"}, {"page": "workload", "label": "Workload management", "id": "b509", "title": "Sterile cockpit", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b509\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Sterile cockpit</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b510\">During phases of work that are critical or high risk, avoid all non-essential conversation and non-task-related activities or noise</li><li id=\"b511\">Operators should only have safety-critical or mission-relevant discussions</li><li id=\"b512\">An enforced distraction-free environment is known as a ‘sterile cockpit’</li><li id=\"b513\">These allow focus and concentration on high-risk tasks</li><li id=\"b514\">Examples include anaesthetic induction, the instrument or swab count in the operating room, drawing up opioid drugs, patient handover, difficult parts of an operation, working in dangerous circumstances.</li><li id=\"b515\">Say it out loud so the rule is shared, not in private: <i>“Can we have a sterile cockpit until we've finished the count, please. Thank you!”</i></li></ul><p id=\"b517\"><b><i>Remember:</i></b> The Sterile Cockpit tool does not prevent people from speaking up about safety. Its precisely there so people can flag safety concerns without being drowned out by the background noise.</p></div></aside>"}, {"page": "workload", "label": "Workload management", "id": "b521", "title": "Dual operators", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b521\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Dual operators</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b522\">When doing high-risk / high-intensity work split the roles: one to do, one to think (monitor)</li><li id=\"b523\">The acting operator drives the task; the monitoring operator watches the whole picture, the clock and the patient.</li><li id=\"b524\">The monitor must be free of hands-on work, otherwise they are not monitoring properly.</li><li id=\"b525\">Plan to swap roles deliberately before the acting operator saturates their cognitive capacity</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"page": "workload", "label": "Workload management", "id": "b527", "title": "Work to standard", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b527\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Work to standard</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"dash\"><li id=\"b528\">Create simple routines and standardised flows, so that routine cases require no active effort or repetition (for example, use templates whenever possible)</li><li id=\"b529\">Write the routine down as a checklist to execute a standard operating procedure (SOP).</li><li id=\"b530\">Keep it short enough to be used.</li><li id=\"b531\">Standardise the layout as well as the steps - same trolley, same order, same place, every time.</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"page": "awareness", "label": "Situational awareness", "id": "b557", "title": "The Capacity Bucket", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b557\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The Capacity Bucket</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b558\">The capacity bucket is the simplest way to understand situational awareness:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b559\">Everyone has a capacity bucket. The more things you throw in the bucket, the less space is left for situational awareness. This includes noise, fatigue, the difficult conversation from an hour ago.</li><li id=\"b560\">Situational awareness represents whatever space is left.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b561\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image17.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: the capacity bucket - workload fills cognitive capacity, leaving situational awareness above it.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"674\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p class=\"ref\" id=\"b562\">Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kaafarani H. Am I safe? Surgery. 2022;171(2):556-7. doi:10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007</p></div></aside>"}, {"page": "awareness", "label": "Situational awareness", "id": "b568", "title": "The 2-challenge rule", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b568\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">The 2-challenge rule</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b569\">Assertiveness tools build the situational awareness of someone in a higher hierarchical role who may be cognitively disoriented. They allow a stepwise escalation in assertiveness.</p><p id=\"b570\">They empower individuals to speak up and ultimately take action when someone has lost situational awareness.</p><p id=\"b572\">This can occur in three stages:</p><p id=\"b573\">1. <b>Prompt</b> - nudge or draw attention to something that is not right. Most of the time, people will respond to a prompt and be grateful for it. “By the way, have you noticed that blood vessel?” It’s soft and curious, and it is easy to accept.</p><p id=\"b575\">2. <b>Direct</b> - if you get no response from the prompt, say what needs to be done in a simple and direct way: “Stop cutting there.” This is an unambiguous instruction that names the action.</p><p id=\"b577\">3. <b>Intervene</b> - if someone has not responded to two challenges of increasing assertiveness, it means they have completely lost situational awareness and will not respond to a third; it is time to act.  Gently take over and carry out the intended action yourself. In this situation, you have given fair warning, and you are obliged to escalate. The second unanswered challenge is the trigger.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b580\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image18.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: the 2-challenge rule - prompt, direct, intervene.\" width=\"1274\" height=\"463\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><p id=\"b582\">This approach works only in an organisation that has adopted this policy and fosters a culture acceptable to the entire workforce.</p></div></aside>"}, {"page": "decisions", "label": "Decision making and problem solving", "id": "b607", "title": "T-DODAR Analytic Problem Solving", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b607\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">T-DODAR Analytic Problem Solving</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b608\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image20.webp\" alt=\"Diagram: T-DODAR steps - time, diagnosis, options, decide, assign and act, review - repeated if it did not work.\" width=\"1273\" height=\"364\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure></div></aside>"}, {"page": "decisions", "label": "Decision making and problem solving", "id": "b616", "title": "Innovative Problem Solving", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b616\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Innovative Problem Solving</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"cycle\" id=\"b617\"><li>Problem</li><li>Idea</li><li>Prototype</li><li>Test &amp; Trial</li><li>Fail &amp; Learn</li><li>Adapt / Adopt</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "leadership", "label": "Leadership, followership and teamwork", "id": "b681", "title": "How to improve your leadership game", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b681\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How to improve your leadership game</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b682\">Safety first: you are the last line of defence between harm and your people</li><li id=\"b683\">Lead by example</li><li id=\"b684\">Set the tone for asking question, challenging decisions and speaking up</li><li id=\"b685\">Keep your word and promises</li><li id=\"b686\">Be kind &amp; fair</li><li id=\"b687\">Take responsibility / Take things seriously</li><li id=\"b688\">Give simple and clear directions</li><li id=\"b689\">Keep a good lookout → check &amp; anticipate</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "communication", "label": "Communication", "id": "b720", "title": "How do you improve communication?", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b720\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">How do you improve communication?</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ol class=\"list\"><li id=\"b721\">Listen first and most; speak second and least.</li><li id=\"b722\">Do not interrupt.</li><li id=\"b723\">Use people's names.</li><li id=\"b724\">Keep eye contact; speak face to face.</li><li id=\"b725\">Meet in person or call rather use email in any situation where tone is important or people might misunderstand intention.</li><li id=\"b726\">Use open body language.</li></ol></div></aside>"}, {"page": "communication", "label": "Communication", "id": "b728", "title": "Crisis communication or briefing", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b728\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Crisis communication or briefing</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><p id=\"b729\">Use the NITS framework to communicate in a crisis:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b730\"><b>N</b>ature of the problem</li><li id=\"b731\"><b>I</b>ntentions: what you are going to do about it?</li><li id=\"b732\"><b>T</b>imeline</li><li id=\"b733\"><b>S</b>pecial instructions: what you would like others to do now</li></ul></div></aside>"}, {"page": "communication", "label": "Communication", "id": "b735", "title": "Closed Loop Communication", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b735\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Closed Loop Communication</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><h4 id=\"b736\">What is closed-loop communication?</h4><p id=\"b737\">Closed-loop communication is the discipline of confirming receipt and understanding of a message by readback and verification, so that no instruction or action is misunderstood.</p><figure class=\"fig\" id=\"b739\"><button class=\"fig-in zoom\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge image\"><img src=\"img/image28.webp\" alt=\"Illustration: closed-loop communication - instruction, readback, verification.\" width=\"1275\" height=\"850\" loading=\"lazy\"></button></figure><h4 id=\"b740\">How does it work?</h4><p id=\"b741\">Closed-loop communication: the receiver confirms what they heard (readback), and the sender checks that it was understood correctly (hearback).</p><h4 id=\"b743\">Example:</h4><p id=\"b744\">During a ward round, the consultant says: \"Can you give 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones, please.\"</p><p id=\"b745\">The nurse replies: \"Just to confirm - 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones in bed 4?\" (<b>readback</b>)</p><p id=\"b746\">The consultant responds: \"That's correct.\" (<b>hearback</b>)</p><p id=\"b747\">The nurse administers the drug and later reports back: \"Paracetamol 1 gram IV given at 14:00, Mrs Jones in bed 4.\" (<b>confirmation</b> that the action is complete)</p></div></aside>"}, {"page": "professional", "label": "Professional standards", "id": "b760", "title": "Hallmarks of professional work", "html": "<aside class=\"co co-tool\" id=\"b760\"><div class=\"co-tag\">TOOL</div><h3 class=\"co-title\">Hallmarks of professional work</h3><div class=\"co-body\"><ul class=\"list\"><li id=\"b761\">Do it properly (do it right)</li><li id=\"b762\">Do it all (do it in the right amount)</li><li id=\"b763\">Do it on time</li><li id=\"b764\">Do it consistently (do it over time)</li></ul></div></aside>"}], "search": [{"p": "welcome", "b": "b1", "k": "text", "t": "Welcome", "x": "App and course written and designed by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b6", "k": "h", "t": "Course philosophy", "x": "Course philosophy"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b7", "k": "text", "t": "Course philosophy", "x": "This course is for everyone, no matter what role or seniority they have in the organisation"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b8", "k": "text", "t": "Course philosophy", "x": "It focuses on teaching people a common language of safety"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b9", "k": "text", "t": "Course philosophy", "x": "It focuses on simple and practical concepts, rather than theory"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b10", "k": "text", "t": "Course philosophy", "x": "It offers tools that you can use to improve your performance in your professional or personal life."}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b12", "k": "h", "t": "How is the material built?", "x": "How is the material built?"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b13", "k": "text", "t": "How is the material built?", "x": "Simple: Plain language, no jargon for its own sake."}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b14", "k": "text", "t": "How is the material built?", "x": "At scale: Built to reach a whole organisation, not a select few."}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b15", "k": "text", "t": "How is the material built?", "x": "Role-agnostic: Applies to every job in the building, clinical and non-clinical"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b16", "k": "text", "t": "How is the material built?", "x": "Micro-learning: Ten-minute chunks. Read one topic at a time, in your own time."}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b18", "k": "h", "t": "Message to the user:", "x": "Message to the user:"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b19", "k": "text", "t": "Message to the user:", "x": "“To err is human”"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b20", "k": "text", "t": "Message to the user:", "x": "Error is part of the human condition. It’s how we avoid, trap and mitigate risks and errors that defines our performance and the safety of our organisation."}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b21", "k": "text", "t": "Message to the user:", "x": "\"Human factors are an operator's manual to the human.\""}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b23", "k": "h", "t": "Source Material", "x": "Source Material"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b24", "k": "text", "t": "Source Material", "x": "All content is derived from:"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "b25", "k": "text", "t": "Source Material", "x": "P Gogalniceanu. “High Performance in Healthcare - Optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery”, King’s College London, 2019"}, {"p": "welcome", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Welcome", "x": "Principles of Human Factors"}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b30", "k": "text", "t": "How to use this guide", "x": "Orientation"}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b31", "k": "text", "t": "How to use this guide", "x": "Welcome to the Principles of Human Factors App. This educational platform was developed by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu for the Simulation and Interactive Learning (SaIL) Centre at Guy’s and St.Thomas NHS Foundation Trust."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b33", "k": "text", "t": "How to use this guide", "x": "There is no fixed order, and nothing is locked. Open what you need, use it, and close it."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b34", "k": "h", "t": "Three ways in", "x": "Three ways in"}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b35", "k": "text", "t": "Three ways in", "x": "Search - press / from anywhere and type a word: fatigue, checklist, bias. Search covers every topic, tool and glossary entry."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b36", "k": "text", "t": "Three ways in", "x": "Index - the left-hand list is grouped the way the subject is: foundations, then technical, cognitive and behavioural skills."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b37", "k": "text", "t": "Three ways in", "x": "Toolkits - every practical tool in the guide, pulled onto one page. Start here if you have a problem in front of you right now."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "b39", "k": "note", "t": "What gets recorded", "x": "Nothing. This page does not track which topics you have opened - there is no progress to pick up and no test to pass. Open what you need."}, {"p": "how-to-use", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "How to use this guide", "x": "1. How to use this guide"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b44", "k": "h", "t": "Aim", "x": "Aim"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b45", "k": "text", "t": "Aim", "x": "This course and App aim to introduce participants to the principles and common language of human factors, in order to facilitate patient safety and operational efficiency across GSTT."}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b46", "k": "h", "t": "Learning Objectives", "x": "Learning Objectives"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b47", "k": "text", "t": "Learning Objectives", "x": "To define what human factors (HF) are and how they work"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b48", "k": "text", "t": "Learning Objectives", "x": "To introduce participants to basic HF concepts"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "b49", "k": "text", "t": "Learning Objectives", "x": "To develop a knowledge of how technical, cognitive and behavioural skills can be used to create safety and security"}, {"p": "aims", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Aims, objectives and outcomes", "x": "2. Aims, objectives and outcomes"}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b53", "k": "h", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "What affects human performance?"}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b54", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Five factors affect human performance. These give rise to both quality and safety, as well as risk, threats and errors:"}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b55", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Technical and non-technical (human factors) skills of the operator"}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b56", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Operator condition: physical, cognitive and emotional."}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b57", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Culture (personal and organisational): values, attitudes, habits and identities."}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b58", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Systems: the people, places, tools and rules available to do work."}, {"p": "performance", "b": "b59", "k": "text", "t": "What affects human performance?", "x": "Task: complexity, magnitude and urgency"}, {"p": "performance", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Human performance", "x": "3. Human Performance and Course Structure"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b66", "k": "h", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "Operator condition:"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b67", "k": "text", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "We can only perform tasks to the extent our biological condition allows."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b68", "k": "text", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "We all have limitations which are impacted by three factors:"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b69", "k": "text", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "Physical status: tiredness, dehydration, hunger, medical conditions, medications, etc."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b70", "k": "text", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "Cognitive status:  cognitive overload (thinking about too many things or thinking for too long) - or its opposite, monotony (boredom)."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b71", "k": "text", "t": "Operator condition:", "x": "Emotional status: stress, anger, fear or moral injury."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b73", "k": "case", "t": "Human factors and aviation accidents", "x": "70–80% of civil and military aviation accidents have been reported to involve human error. References: Wiegmann DA, Shappell SA. A human error analysis of commercial aviation accidents using the Human Factors Analysis and Classification System (HFACS). Washington (DC): Federal Aviation Administration, Office of Aviation Medicine; 2001. Report No.: DOT/FAA/AM-01/3. Available from: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b77", "k": "case", "t": "Human factors and clinical error", "x": "In one Dutch study of 21 hospitals, human causes contributed to 61% of adverse events. Smits M, Zegers M, Groenewegen PP, Timmermans DRM, Zwaan L, van der Wal G, et al. Exploring the causes of adverse events in hospitals and potential prevention strategies. Qual Saf Health Care. 2010;19(5):e5. doi:10.1136/qshc.2008.030726."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b82", "k": "h", "t": "Deconditioning in healthcare", "x": "Deconditioning in healthcare"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b83", "k": "h", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b84", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Night shifts / Long shifts"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b85", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Long commutes"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b86", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Operations that overrun"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b87", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Working with no natural light"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b88", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "No access to rest areas"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b89", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "No water or healthy food"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b90", "k": "text", "t": "What physical factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Medical problems or medications"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b92", "k": "h", "t": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b93", "k": "text", "t": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Distractions"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b94", "k": "text", "t": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Multi-tasking"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b95", "k": "text", "t": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Working overtime"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b96", "k": "text", "t": "What cognitive factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Weekend work"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b98", "k": "h", "t": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b99", "k": "text", "t": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Working in isolated or high-risk roles"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b100", "k": "text", "t": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Conflict and work politics"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b101", "k": "text", "t": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "Family instability"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b103", "k": "text", "t": "What emotional factors can impair safe performance?", "x": "If you are exposed to any of the above risk factors – beware! You may be at risk of making an error."}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b107", "k": "h", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b108", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Increased reaction time"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b109", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Reduced attentiveness"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b110", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Impaired memory"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b111", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Withdrawn mood"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b112", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Loss of empathy"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b113", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Risk-taking behaviour"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b114", "k": "text", "t": "What fatigue and stress do to performance?", "x": "Incivility or conflict-generating behaviour"}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b116", "k": "note", "t": "Good to know about fatigue…", "x": "Every item on that list can be easily missed by the person experiencing it. Fatigue degrades the very faculty you would use to notice you are fatigued. This is why you need to think ahead, take feedback from colleagues, set boundaries, have guardrails and rely on systems being put in place. "}, {"p": "operator", "b": "b119", "k": "tool", "t": "3 Principles for optimising operator condition", "x": "You have to be ‘fit for the job’. Optimise yourself to optimise your outcomes. Think body, mind and emotions.  Never do safety-critical work if you are acutely unwell or have not slept. Manage your energy: eat, sleep, hydrate, move, socialise. "}, {"p": "operator", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Operator condition", "x": "4. Operator (biological) condition"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b125", "k": "text", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "Our ability to think calmly and rationally is the first we lose when we are faced with a surprising (‘startling’) or threatening event."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b126", "k": "text", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "There is nothing wrong or shameful about this. It happens to all of us. It’s part of the human defence reaction."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b127", "k": "text", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "When threatened, we switch from logical to emotional thinking - that is just how our brains are wired."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b128", "k": "text", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "This phenomenon has different names: “the fog of war”, “the red mist” or the “amygdala hijack”"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b129", "k": "text", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "This is good to know, because it predicts how we respond to a sudden emergency – and it’s not always in a good way!"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b131", "k": "note", "t": "Emotions beat reason every time. ", "x": ""}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b134", "k": "h", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "The startled human:"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b135", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "Startle is a reaction where your emotions take over your thinking. You stop being rational and logical. Instead, you will display one of four (dangerous) fear or threat reactions:"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b136", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "1. Fight - impulsive action or aggressive behaviour. Doing something just for the sake of doing it."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b137", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "2. Flight - leaving or withdrawing - physically or by handing the problem to someone else."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b138", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "3. Freeze - inaction. You just stop functioning or engaging."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b139", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "4. Fawn - trying to appease others rather than advocating your point of view, speaking truth to power or standing up for yourself."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b142", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "Remember: Being affected by the startle syndrome is not a character flaw - it’s part of our deep-seated behavioural code."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b143", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "Our job is to recognise startle and control it as best as we can. We will not always be successful."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b144", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "This matters when we observe sudden or atypical unprofessional behaviours. In such cases, we must ask – “Are we not just seeing a startled or stressed individual?” “Are there stressors, threats or fears that are driving these unusual behaviours?”"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b145", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "It’s critical we differentiate between the startle effect and underperformance."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b146", "k": "text", "t": "The startled human:", "x": "This does not mean we justify or tolerate incivility or unprofessionalism."}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b148", "k": "tool", "t": "Startle-management toolkit", "x": "How do you manage your startle syndrome? Startle Tool 1: ‘Stop and Sit on your hands” “Sit on your hands”. Fight the impulse to jump to solutions. The first instinct is rarely the right one. Stop - do nothing for a moment. Very few emergencies are made worse by three seconds of doing nothing. Distract the distraction: count to 30, take 10 deep breaths, make a hot drink, go for a walk, play with your watch. Stay calm and methodical: Re-orient using a structure you already know by heart. Startle Tool 2: Have a mental model or cognitive re-orientation tool you can fall back on. Some examples of mental models or tools to re-orient in a startled situation: ABCD (Medical) - Airway - Breathing - Circulation - Disability ABCD (Surgical) - Access - Bleeding control - Contamination/infection control - Decide: damage control vs definitive care - Exit (damage control) or Escalate (get help in definitive care) PMC Priority actions: critical tasks Move: move people, mobilise resources, modify environments Communicate 4Ps Patient Place Procedure Personnel (staff)"}, {"p": "startle", "b": "b184", "k": "note", "t": "Cognitive tools exist not just to aid diagnosis and provide initial medical care. They provide the startled brain a familiar rail to run on until it can think logically again.", "x": ""}, {"p": "startle", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Startle syndrome", "x": "What is ‘Startle Syndrome’?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b188", "k": "h", "t": "What is culture?", "x": "What is culture?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b189", "k": "text", "t": "What is culture?", "x": "Culture refers to the values, attitudes and habits that shape how we feel, think and behave."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b190", "k": "text", "t": "What is culture?", "x": "We acquire culture from the social environment we grow up in, live in and work in."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b191", "k": "text", "t": "What is culture?", "x": "Often, we are unaware of how our culture affects us."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b195", "k": "h", "t": "Culture determines how we answer the following questions:", "x": "Culture determines how we answer the following questions:"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b196", "k": "text", "t": "Culture determines how we answer the following questions:", "x": "What is normal to you?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b197", "k": "text", "t": "Culture determines how we answer the following questions:", "x": "What does good look like?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b198", "k": "text", "t": "Culture determines how we answer the following questions:", "x": "How should things be done?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b200", "k": "h", "t": "The problem with culture", "x": "The problem with culture"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b201", "k": "text", "t": "The problem with culture", "x": "It cannot be seen."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b202", "k": "text", "t": "The problem with culture", "x": "It cannot be measured."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b203", "k": "text", "t": "The problem with culture", "x": "It cannot be changed easily."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b205", "k": "h", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "What determines culture?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b206", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Family"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b207", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Social group"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b208", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Professional group"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b209", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Ethnicity"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b210", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Nationality"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b211", "k": "text", "t": "What determines culture?", "x": "Our attitudes to continuous learning and self-growth"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b213", "k": "h", "t": "What actually changes culture?", "x": "What actually changes culture?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b214", "k": "h", "t": "The three Cs", "x": "The three Cs"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b215", "k": "text", "t": "The three Cs", "x": "1. Catastrophes - shocks and harmful events. This is an effective way to change culture, but it is also the worst - because it comes at great cost."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b216", "k": "text", "t": "The three Cs", "x": "2. Champions - credible role models who lead by example."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b217", "k": "text", "t": "The three Cs", "x": "3. Case studies - storytelling. Humans are emotional before they are rational. A well-told story changes how we feel, and therefore how we think, decide, and behave. A story can change what data, reason, and logic often can’t."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b219", "k": "case", "t": "Titanic (1912)", "x": "A shock large enough to rewrite maritime rules: lifeboats for all on board, continuous radio watch, an ice patrol. Nothing about the safety culture was new that night. The catastrophe simply made the existing arguments impossible to ignore."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b222", "k": "case", "t": "Rosa Parks (1955)", "x": "On 1 December 1955, Rosa Parks, a seamstress and experienced civil rights activist, refused an order to give up her seat to a passenger on a segregated bus in Montgomery, Alabama. Her arrest helped spark the Montgomery bus boycott: 381 days of collective action that challenged racial segregation. Her courage became a catalyst for change because an organised community rallied behind it.  Champions change culture by challenging accepted norms, having the courage to go against the grain and giving others the confidence to act. Lasting change comes when that example becomes a shared commitment, supported by collective action."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b226", "k": "h", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "A few cultures you should know about:"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b228", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Culture of safety - “Safety first” - first do no harm; be safe and secure in everything you do."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b230", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Culture of learning – ‘Every day is a school day’. We need to be curious: learn from the past, keep up with current innovations, and think about how we can improve the future."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b232", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Culture of resilience - “Failures will happen - better be ready to prevent, respond and recover.”"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b234", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Culture of justice - When failure occurs, draw a line between acceptable and unacceptable behaviour."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b235", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Was this bad luck, a genuinely innocent mistake or reckless behaviour?"}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b236", "k": "text", "t": "A few cultures you should know about:", "x": "Only then can you decide the outcome: disciplinary action, training, or support."}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b239", "k": "note", "t": "A just culture is not a “no-blame culture”.", "x": "We must all be responsible and accountable. "}, {"p": "culture", "b": "b242", "k": "tool", "t": "Just Culture", "x": "When an unintended outcome occurs, we need to understand why. This helps us learn and avoid it happening again. It’s about safety, not blame. The next person to be harmed could be you, me or someone we care about.  Justice arises from the fair attribution of responsibility rather than looking for someone to blame.  It is important to first ask “Why did this happen?” rather than “Who did it?” Only then can we debrief fairly, transparently and openly in order to learn. A just culture does not mean we always find systems and circumstances to blame instead of holding individuals accountable. A just culture asks a prior question - was this a genuine error, a risky choice, or reckless behaviour? - and applies a different response to each.  Staff will report threats and errors only when they know they will be treated fairly and trust a system that holds people accountable when it should. In a just culture, accountability is attributed to individuals fairly and proportionally.  In an innocent mistake, such as a system failure or misunderstanding, support is provided to the individual involved In an error of inexperience, lack of skill or knowledge, training or coaching is provided If the error occurred due to negligence, inappropriate risk-taking behaviour or wilful misdoing, appropriate disciplinary action may be considered. "}, {"p": "culture", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Culture", "x": "5. Culture"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b265", "k": "h", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Key question: What makes up a health system?"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b266", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Humans do not perform tasks in isolation. They perform in specific work environments, also known as ecosystems."}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b268", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "A health system consists of four elements:"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b269", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "People – the humans carrying out the tasks"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b271", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Places – the physical environment where work is carried out (e.g. the ward, the clinic, operating theatre or GP practice)"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b273", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Tools – the equipment and technology that carry out the work. These include both hardware and software. Examples include computer stations, medical devices, electronic medical records, funding, and other assets or resources."}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b275", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Rules - the policies, protocols, procedures and playbooks that guide how we work; this can also be defined as the ‘organisational’ environment"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b277", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Threats, errors and harm usually arise from one of the four categories mentioned above: people, places, tools and rules."}, {"p": "systems", "b": "b279", "k": "text", "t": "Key question: What makes up a health system?", "x": "Reference: How crises work: A model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. International Journal of Surgery 104:p 106711, August 2022. P Gogalniceanu et al"}, {"p": "systems", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Health systems", "x": "6. Health Systems"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b282", "k": "text", "t": "Threats, errors, harm and crises", "x": "Key question: Is “zero harm” an aspiration or a realistic goal?"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b284", "k": "h", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "How does harm occur?"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b285", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Harm is not always an accident."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b286", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Harm occurs through a sequence of events, known as the ‘crisis chain’"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b289", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Threats are any risk factors that make errors more likely. Threats are conditions that push towards failure. Examples:"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b290", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "People: tired, untrained or unwell staff"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b291", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Places: a ward that has no clean water supply"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b292", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Tools: faulty infusion pumps or X-ray equipment"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b293", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Rules: rotas that do not allow sufficient rest times between shifts"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b295", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Errors are unintended outcomes – also known as system ‘failures’.  Errors themselves don’t necessarily lead to harm. Errors can be either inconsequential (nothing bad happens) or consequential (something bad happens). However, if you do nothing about an error, it may lead to harm the next time it happens."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b297", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Harm describes errors that negatively impact someone or something, for example, a patient, a member of staff, or the organisation."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b299", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Crises are situations where harms have not been correctly mitigated. This creates novel, uncertain, time-sensitive, and safety-critical situations. If not managed appropriately, crises can have catastrophic or terminal outcomes. Crises are characterised by multiple competing priorities, making the problem and solution dynamic and unclear."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b300", "k": "text", "t": "How does harm occur?", "x": "Reference: How crises work: A model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. International Journal of Surgery 104:p 106711, August 2022. P Gogalniceanu et al"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b302", "k": "h", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b303", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "Health systems are tightly coupled - meaning they contain many moving parts that are interconnected and interdependent."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b304", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "A tightly coupled system has little slack"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b305", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "It is routinely exposed to multiple threats"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b306", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "It undertakes complex tasks"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b307", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "A change in one part can impact every other part"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b308", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "This means it is easy to initiate a crisis chain in healthcare"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b309", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "Because we work in complex systems, we can't eliminate harm completely. Instead, we should aim to prevent it, respond to it, reduce its impact and recover from it to create even safer operators and systems."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b310", "k": "text", "t": "Why are Hospitals and Health Systems prone to errors?", "x": "Getting things right the first time is therefore essential"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b312", "k": "note", "t": "Safety mindset - Errors happen all the time. It’s not a matter of if, it is a matter of when a major failure will occur. Are you ready for it? What would you need to change or prepare right now, if today was the day? Always ask, ‘what if?’.", "x": ""}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b314", "k": "h", "t": "How do we model and analyse error?", "x": "How do we model and analyse error?"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b315", "k": "h", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Bow-tie analysis:"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b316", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "A bow-tie analysis is a simple way of looking at the cause and effect of any hazard or failure - what leads to an error on one side, and what is the impact (harms) on the other."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b318", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Diagram: Bow-tie: threats and traps, barriers, error, mitigations, harms"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b322", "k": "h", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Managing Crises:"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b324", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Barriers: Anything that stops or avoids a threat from becoming an error: a checklist, a second operator, a design that prevents wrong or incorrect use."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b326", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Traps: Anything that stops an error that has occurred from leading to harm. For example, having two surgeons operating at the same time or equipment redundancy"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b328", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Mitigations: Anything that stabilises a critical situation, reduces the impact of harm, and buys you more time to fix a safety-critical problem. Once harm has occurred, it’s all about damage control, buying time and stabilising the situation."}, {"p": "crises", "b": "b332", "k": "text", "t": "Bow-tie analysis:", "x": "Reference: Avoid, Trap, and Mitigate: Development of an Evidence-Based Crisis Management Framework in Surgery. Journal of the American College of Surgeons 233(4):p 536e1, October 2021. Gogalniceanu et al"}, {"p": "crises", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Threats, errors, harm and crises", "x": "Threats, Errors, Harm and Crises"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b336", "k": "h", "t": "What are Human factors?", "x": "What are Human factors?"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b337", "k": "text", "t": "What are Human factors?", "x": "Human factors (HF) science is all about optimising human performance to undertake complex tasks in fragile environments."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b339", "k": "text", "t": "What are Human factors?", "x": "Put another way:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b340", "k": "text", "t": "What are Human factors?", "x": "Make the best use of all the available resources to obtain the optimum outcome"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b342", "k": "text", "t": "What are Human factors?", "x": "“Human Factors: doing the best that you can with what you have to get what you want”"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b346", "k": "h", "t": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:", "x": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b347", "k": "text", "t": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:", "x": "1. The world is not perfect."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b348", "k": "text", "t": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:", "x": "2. Error is part of the human condition."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b349", "k": "text", "t": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:", "x": "3. If you do enough high-volume, high-risk work, harm can occur."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b351", "k": "text", "t": "Human factors science acknowledges three facts:", "x": "The goal of human factors is to prevent errors and harm at all costs, but also to prepare to respond and recover should harm occur."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b353", "k": "h", "t": "Solution:", "x": "Solution:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b354", "k": "text", "t": "Solution:", "x": "We can all learn the skills, attitudes and behaviours that can help us better prepare, prevent, respond and recover from harm"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b355", "k": "text", "t": "Solution:", "x": "Our goal is to avoid harm, and when harm happens, to reduce its impact"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b357", "k": "h", "t": "Human Factors values:", "x": "Human Factors values:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b358", "k": "text", "t": "Human Factors values:", "x": "Practical, Pragmatic, Proactive"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b362", "k": "h", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "How do we think about Human Factors?"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b364", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Human factors are based on three pillars, each consisting of 3 domains."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b365", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Pillar 1: Technical skills:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b366", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Specialist subject-matter knowledge"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b367", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Technical skills and experience"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b368", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Technology: tools and equipment"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b370", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Pillar 2: Cognitive skills:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b371", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Workload management"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b372", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Situational awareness"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b373", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Problem solving"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b375", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Pillar 3: Behavioural skills:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b376", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Leadership & teamwork"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b377", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Communication"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b378", "k": "text", "t": "How do we think about Human Factors?", "x": "Professional standards"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b382", "k": "h", "t": "Human Factors Model", "x": "Human Factors Model"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b385", "k": "h", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b386", "k": "text", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "The System (environment) we work in"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b387", "k": "text", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "The Condition of the human operators"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b388", "k": "text", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "Culture: values, attitudes and habits we acquire from our environments"}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b390", "k": "text", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "When performance is lacking in one of these 9 domains, the remaining 8 can compensate."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "b392", "k": "text", "t": "These skills can be enhanced or degraded by three factors:", "x": "For example, if you lack knowledge or expertise in a particular area of work (technical skill), a good leader would have the situational awareness (cognitive skill) to realise this and leverage the experience of other team members through effective teamwork (behavioural skill) to make sure you are supported and the quality of the work is not affected."}, {"p": "human-factors", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Human factors", "x": "8. Human Factors"}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b400", "k": "h", "t": "Technology", "x": "Technology"}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b401", "k": "text", "t": "Technology", "x": "Technology refers to the tools, equipment and innovations that extend human capability and address human needs."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b402", "k": "text", "t": "Technology", "x": "However, technology is only as good as its use, design and integration."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b404", "k": "h", "t": "Technology can create complexity.", "x": "Technology can create complexity."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b405", "k": "text", "t": "Technology can create complexity.", "x": "In human factors, simple works best."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b406", "k": "text", "t": "Technology can create complexity.", "x": "When new or complex technology is introduced, humans are more likely to make a mistake."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b407", "k": "text", "t": "Technology can create complexity.", "x": "This is why performance has a learning curve – until we build experience, we are more vulnerable to making a mistake."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b409", "k": "h", "t": "Socio-Technical Interface", "x": "Socio-Technical Interface"}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b410", "k": "text", "t": "Socio-Technical Interface", "x": "The interaction between a human and a machine is known as the socio-technical interface."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b411", "k": "text", "t": "Socio-Technical Interface", "x": "Errors are likely to occur at this interface."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b412", "k": "text", "t": "Socio-Technical Interface", "x": "Barriers, traps, and mitigations need to be inserted to address these risks."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b414", "k": "tool", "t": "How to fix technology risk", "x": "Safety by design - make it easy for people to do things right and hard to do things wrong. Training - mentorship, simulation and high case volume before independent use. Responsible use - an ethical duty to use the tool for what it is for, and to stop when it is the wrong tool. Governance and legislation - the rules that hold when enthusiasm runs ahead of evidence."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b423", "k": "case", "t": "Shape-coded cockpit controls (Aviation · Second World War)", "x": "As aircraft grew more complex, crews began retracting the landing gear on the runway instead of the flaps. The fix was not more training or more discipline: the landing gear lever was given a small wheel on the end and the flaps lever a flat paddle. You could tell the controls apart by hand, in the dark, under stress. This is safety by design in one object - and why theatre kit, connectors, and infusion lines are increasingly shaped so they physically cannot be misconnected."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b426", "k": "h", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "SHELL Model"}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b427", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "The SHELL Model illustrates how the socio-technical interface operates."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b428", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "S - Software - The intangibles: policies, protocols, procedures, the electronic record, the way the list is written."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b429", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "H - Hardware - Equipment, instruments, monitors, pumps, buildings, furniture."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b430", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "E - Environment - Light, noise, heat, space, time of day, interruption load."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b431", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "L - Liveware (you) - The person at the centre: their skill, condition, attention and limits."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b432", "k": "text", "t": "SHELL Model", "x": "L - Liveware (others) - Everyone else - the team, other specialties, the patient and their family."}, {"p": "technology", "b": "b437", "k": "note", "t": "\"Humans are at the same time the strongest and the weakest link in any system.\"", "x": "Technology always requires human supervision.  Technology also compensates for human limitations. "}, {"p": "technology", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Technology", "x": "9. Technical Skills"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b444", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge, skill and experience", "x": "Without skill, knowledge and experience, no amount of human factors training can create safe and highly performant outcomes"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b446", "k": "h", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "Knowledge"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b447", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "Knowledge requires study. You need to know the facts, understand how they relate, and build a mental model of how they work in practice. You also need to understand how they fail, so that you can prevent, detect and fix errors once they occur."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b449", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "You can think of knowledge in 3 categories:"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b450", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "Form: how things are made or put together"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b451", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "Function: how they work"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b452", "k": "text", "t": "Knowledge", "x": "Failure: how they break down"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b455", "k": "h", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "Skill and Experience"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b456", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "It is not enough to know. You also have to be able to do. You have to be a practitioner. This requires real-world experience and case volume."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b457", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "Often learning occurs by doing."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b458", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "Skills need practice, rehearsal, and refinement."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b459", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "Failure is part of learning, but it must occur in controlled environments, with adequate supervision, mentorship and feedback."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b460", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "This makes simulation an essential tool – it allows you to fail safely and learn from your mistakes without putting anyone at risk or in danger."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b461", "k": "text", "t": "Skill and Experience", "x": "The process of learning from failures is called “failing well” or “failing forward”"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b463", "k": "h", "t": "Deliberate Practice", "x": "Deliberate Practice"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b464", "k": "text", "t": "Deliberate Practice", "x": "Deliberate practice improves performance through repetition, feedback, and iterative refinement."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b465", "k": "text", "t": "Deliberate Practice", "x": "Practice does not make perfect performance. Perfect practice makes perfect performance."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b466", "k": "text", "t": "Deliberate Practice", "x": "This is why personal discipline, determination and mentorship are essential."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b468", "k": "tool", "t": "for Subject Matter Expertise", "x": "Know first principles – the universal ‘rules of the road’ Have frameworks and mental models Do the basic things exceedingly well.   Practice, have mentorship, ask for feedback."}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "b474", "k": "note", "t": "The difference between amateurs and professionals is:", "x": "Attention to detail Ownership of the problem, solution, and outcome Follow-up and continuous learning"}, {"p": "knowledge", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Knowledge, skill and experience", "x": "Knowledge · Skill · Experience"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b483", "k": "h", "t": "Workload management", "x": "Workload management"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b484", "k": "h", "t": "Principles:", "x": "Principles:"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b485", "k": "text", "t": "Principles:", "x": "You can think or you can execute - you can’t do both well at the same time."}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b486", "k": "text", "t": "Principles:", "x": "The higher the workload, the lower your situational awareness."}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b487", "k": "text", "t": "Principles:", "x": "You can do anything, but not everything"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b489", "k": "h", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "What makes work ‘hard’?"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b490", "k": "text", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "Disruptions"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b491", "k": "text", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "Thinking and doing at the same time"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b492", "k": "text", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "New or complex tasks"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b493", "k": "text", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "Conflict or uncertainty"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b494", "k": "text", "t": "What makes work ‘hard’?", "x": "Fear"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b499", "k": "h", "t": "How do you manage high workloads?", "x": "How do you manage high workloads?"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b500", "k": "text", "t": "How do you manage high workloads?", "x": "Offload tasks & delegate appropriately"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b501", "k": "text", "t": "How do you manage high workloads?", "x": "Make a “To do” List"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b502", "k": "text", "t": "How do you manage high workloads?", "x": "Prioritise items: differentiate between urgent and important tasks"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b503", "k": "text", "t": "How do you manage high workloads?", "x": "Use the Eisenhower Matrix (below) to address tasks"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b505", "k": "tool", "t": "Eisenhower prioritisation matrix", "x": ""}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b509", "k": "tool", "t": "Sterile cockpit", "x": "- During phases of work that are critical or high risk, avoid all non-essential conversation and non-task-related activities or noise  - Operators should only have safety-critical or mission-relevant discussions  - An enforced distraction-free environment is known as a ‘sterile cockpit’ - These allow focus and concentration on high-risk tasks - Examples include anaesthetic induction, the instrument or swab count in the operating room, drawing up opioid drugs, patient handover, difficult parts of an operation, working in dangerous circumstances. - Say it out loud so the rule is shared, not in private: “Can we have a sterile cockpit until we've finished the count, please. Thank you!” Remember: The Sterile Cockpit tool does not prevent people from speaking up about safety. Its precisely there so people can flag safety concerns without being drowned out by the background noise."}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b519", "k": "note", "t": "The ‘sterile cockpit’ principles is borrowed from commercial aviation, where the rule below 10,000 feet is that nothing is discussed except flying the aircraft.", "x": ""}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b521", "k": "tool", "t": "Dual operators", "x": "- When doing high-risk / high-intensity work split the roles: one to do, one to think (monitor) - The acting operator drives the task; the monitoring operator watches the whole picture, the clock and the patient. - The monitor must be free of hands-on work, otherwise they are not monitoring properly. - Plan to swap roles deliberately before the acting operator saturates their cognitive capacity"}, {"p": "workload", "b": "b527", "k": "tool", "t": "Work to standard", "x": "- Create simple routines and standardised flows, so that routine cases require no active effort or repetition (for example, use templates whenever possible) - Write the routine down as a checklist to execute a standard operating procedure (SOP). - Keep it short enough to be used.  - Standardise the layout as well as the steps - same trolley, same order, same place, every time."}, {"p": "workload", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Workload management", "x": "11. Cognitive Skills"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b536", "k": "h", "t": "Cognitive Capacity", "x": "Cognitive Capacity"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b537", "k": "text", "t": "Cognitive Capacity", "x": "Cognitive capacity is your mental ‘bandwidth’"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b538", "k": "text", "t": "Cognitive Capacity", "x": "If you want to think better, reduce workload and increase situational awareness"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b540", "k": "h", "t": "Situational awareness", "x": "Situational awareness"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b541", "k": "text", "t": "Situational awareness", "x": "Situational awareness (SA) is your ability to notice, understand and think ahead"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b546", "k": "h", "t": "Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)", "x": "Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b547", "k": "text", "t": "Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)", "x": "Notice → What? What just happened? What is actually in front of me?"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b548", "k": "text", "t": "Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)", "x": "Understand → So what? What does that mean for this patient, this list, this team?"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b549", "k": "text", "t": "Situational Awareness has three levels (NUTA)", "x": "Think ahead → Now what? Where is this going, and what will I need in five minutes, 5 hours, or 5 days from now?"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b551", "k": "h", "t": "What decays situational awareness?", "x": "What decays situational awareness?"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b552", "k": "text", "t": "What decays situational awareness?", "x": "Physical factors - fatigue, hunger, dehydration"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b553", "k": "text", "t": "What decays situational awareness?", "x": "Cognitive factors - high workload, distractions"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b554", "k": "text", "t": "What decays situational awareness?", "x": "Emotional factors - stress, fear, anger"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b555", "k": "text", "t": "What decays situational awareness?", "x": "Losing awareness means losing the ability to monitor, to process, or to predict - and you generally lose them in that order without noticing any of it."}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b557", "k": "tool", "t": "The Capacity Bucket", "x": "The capacity bucket is the simplest way to understand situational awareness: Everyone has a capacity bucket. The more things you throw in the bucket, the less space is left for situational awareness. This includes noise, fatigue, the difficult conversation from an hour ago.  Situational awareness represents whatever space is left.   Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kaafarani H. Am I safe? Surgery. 2022;171(2):556-7. doi:10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007"}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b565", "k": "case", "t": "Eastern Air Lines Flight 401 (Florida Everglades · 29 December 1972)", "x": "A Lockheed TriStar approaching Miami showed an unlit landing gear indicator. All three flight crew turned their attention to a two-dollar light bulb. Somewhere in that discussion, the autopilot disconnected, and the aircraft entered a gentle descent nobody was watching. It flew into the Everglades. 101 people died; 75 survived. The gear was down the whole time. The classic fixation accident: a whole crew, fully awake, entirely competent, and with a perfectly functional aircraft – BUT with 100% of their attention on 1% of the problem."}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "b568", "k": "tool", "t": "The 2-challenge rule", "x": "Assertiveness tools build the situational awareness of someone in a higher hierarchical role who may be cognitively disoriented. They allow a stepwise escalation in assertiveness. They empower individuals to speak up and ultimately take action when someone has lost situational awareness. This can occur in three stages: 1. Prompt - nudge or draw attention to something that is not right. Most of the time, people will respond to a prompt and be grateful for it. “By the way, have you noticed that blood vessel?” It’s soft and curious, and it is easy to accept. 2. Direct - if you get no response from the prompt, say what needs to be done in a simple and direct way: “Stop cutting there.” This is an unambiguous instruction that names the action. 3. Intervene - if someone has not responded to two challenges of increasing assertiveness, it means they have completely lost situational awareness and will not respond to a third; it is time to act.  Gently take over and carry out the intended action yourself. In this situation, you have given fair warning, and you are obliged to escalate. The second unanswered challenge is the trigger.  This approach works only in an organisation that has adopted this policy and fosters a culture acceptable to the entire workforce. "}, {"p": "awareness", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Situational awareness", "x": "Situational awareness"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b587", "k": "h", "t": "How do you make good decisions?", "x": "How do you make good decisions?"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b588", "k": "text", "t": "How do you make good decisions?", "x": "Cognitive Space. First, create the mental bandwidth to make good decisions. This is called cognitive capacity. To build it,  reduce workload, increase situational awareness and avoid distractions."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b590", "k": "text", "t": "How do you make good decisions?", "x": "Emergency Protocols / Checklists. Check whether protocols are in place to help you fix the problem you face. Is there an emergency checklist or Emergency Operating Procedure available? If yes, use it. It’s there for a reason. No protocol? You will have to problem-solve."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b592", "k": "text", "t": "How do you make good decisions?", "x": "Analytic problem-solving: work out the solution from scratch"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b594", "k": "h", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b596", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "The T-DODAR tool allows you to think logically through a problem. Each letter stands for a step in the process."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b598", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "T - Time - How long have I got to solve the problem? This answer sets every other answer. Do I need to rush to a quick solution, or can I take my time?"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b599", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "D - Diagnosis - What is actually happening? Gather opinions from the whole team, not just the loudest voice. How bad is it? How might things evolve?"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b600", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "O - Options - What could we do? An option is not a decision. Make a list of all the possible solutions. For each option, list benefits and risks. List alternatives, including the option of doing nothing (BRAN model). Don’t forget to include options you don’t like or that are ‘out of the box’."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b601", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "D - Decide – Analyse all options and choose the single best one. Only one! Name the choice so everyone hears the same plan."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b602", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "A - Assign task and Act – Decide who does what, and by when. Allocate tasks by name."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b603", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems? T-DODAR method", "x": "R - Review - Measure the outcome of your intervention. Use metrics and be specific. Did it work? If not, run the cycle again. Don’t skip this step! Remember it’s a cycle"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b607", "k": "tool", "t": "T-DODAR Analytic Problem Solving", "x": ""}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b610", "k": "h", "t": "How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?", "x": "How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b611", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?", "x": "1. Use First Principles. If you can’t work out how to fix a problem, think of the first principles. One such example: “Never walk away from a sick patient”. These are ‘rules of the road’ that guide actions but can lead to poor outcomes if applied incorrectly."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b612", "k": "text", "t": "How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?", "x": "2. Innovate: This is a trial-and-error approach. You see a problem, develop an idea, and create a prototype solution. Then test an trial that solution in a controlled and safe environment. You test to make it fail; you learn from that failure, then improve the prototype and repeat the cycle until you have a product that can be adopted. This approach has safety limitations in healthcare and should be used only within appropriate ethical, innovation, and governance frameworks."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b613", "k": "h", "t": "How do you solve problems when you don’t know what’s going on?", "x": "Innovation Cycle"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b616", "k": "tool", "t": "Innovative Problem Solving", "x": ""}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b619", "k": "h", "t": "Why do smart people make bad decisions?", "x": "Why do smart people make bad decisions?"}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b620", "k": "text", "t": "Why do smart people make bad decisions?", "x": "Attitudes: Hazardous attitudes - machismo, invincibility, anti-authority, impulsivity, resignation."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b621", "k": "text", "t": "Why do smart people make bad decisions?", "x": "Biases:  shortcuts our brain uses to make quick decisions; they aren’t always right and can get us into trouble.  Examples: recency bias (being swayed by the last case you saw), optimism bias (it will be fine), fixation bias (focusing on one issue, whilst ignoring everything else)."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b622", "k": "text", "t": "Why do smart people make bad decisions?", "x": "Condition: Operator deconditioning - stress, fear, fatigue, overstimulation."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "b626", "k": "note", "t": "A, B, C", "x": "Attitudes, biases, and conditions are not knowledge deficits, which is why more teaching does not fix them.  Address them by leading by example, providing strong role models, challenging them, and looking after the operator."}, {"p": "decisions", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Decision making and problem solving", "x": "Decision making and problem solving"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b632", "k": "h", "t": "Leadership, followership and teamwork", "x": "Leadership, followership and teamwork"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b634", "k": "h", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "What is leadership?"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b635", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "\"Leadership is the ability to drive positive change for those we serve.\""}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b636", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "P Gogalniceanu"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b638", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "\"Management is about doing things right; leadership is about doing the right things.\""}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b639", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "Peter Drucker"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b640", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "“Leaders are changemakers. They take people safely from A to B.”"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b642", "k": "text", "t": "What is leadership?", "x": "Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32–42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b645", "k": "h", "t": "Teamwork & Followership", "x": "Teamwork & Followership"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b646", "k": "text", "t": "Teamwork & Followership", "x": "Leadership, followership and teamwork are three roles that we all need to play."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b647", "k": "text", "t": "Teamwork & Followership", "x": "You should be able to move between roles independent of your experience, seniority or position in the organisation."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b648", "k": "text", "t": "Teamwork & Followership", "x": "You can’t be a good leader if you can’t follow or work as part of a team"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b653", "k": "h", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b654", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "Hierarchy is the role we play in an organisation. It is set and non-negotiable."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b655", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "It’s a normal social structure that helps us operate efficiently."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b656", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "It gives us the legitimacy to do our jobs."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b657", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "Without hierarchy, there is chaos."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b659", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "Authority is how we choose to use the power that our hierarchical position gives us."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b660", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "If we choose to be authoritative and formal, then we use a steep authority gradient"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b661", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "If we are approachable and informal, we are applying a shallow authority gradient"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b662", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "There is no such thing as a flat authority gradient, as we all operate within a hierarchical structure – we all report to someone and receive reports from others."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b664", "k": "text", "t": "What is the difference between hierarchy and authority?", "x": "Reference: Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32–42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b666", "k": "h", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "How do we get the right authority gradient?"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b667", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "In most situations, we should use a shallow authority gradient - that is, we should not rely on positional power to get things done. Instead, we should use clear explanations, good morale and motivation to encourage people to follow our plans or instructions."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b668", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "This means including all team members in discussions, involving them in decisions, asking for their opinions, listening to their views, and working collaboratively to achieve the best possible outcome. Compromise is a key part of this, unless you are compromising on safety, in which case you need to draw a clear line."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b669", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "The authority gradient can steepen when safety is at risk, incivility occurs, norms are deliberately broken, or disrespectful behaviour is seen. In such cases, we can be more directive or instructional."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b670", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "Getting the authority gradient wrong damages trust, respect and relationships, and ultimately compromises teamwork, followership and leadership."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b671", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "Remember: adjust your authority gradient to the task's complexity and risk, as well as to the team you are leading."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b672", "k": "text", "t": "How do we get the right authority gradient?", "x": "Be an adaptive leader."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b673", "k": "h", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b674", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Competence: can do the job and deliver results – does the basic things exceedingly well."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b675", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Character: does what is right even when it’s hard, and no one is looking (integrity)."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b676", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Courage: acts responsibly in the face of adversity"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b677", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Communication: clear, simple, consistent."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b678", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Curiosity: is willing to learn and try new things."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b679", "k": "text", "t": "What we want from leaders? The 6 Cs", "x": "Charisma: motivates people to follow them."}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "b681", "k": "tool", "t": "How to improve your leadership game", "x": "Safety first: you are the last line of defence between harm and your people Lead by example Set the tone for asking question, challenging decisions and speaking up Keep your word and promises Be kind & fair Take responsibility / Take things seriously Give simple and clear directions Keep a good lookout → check & anticipate"}, {"p": "leadership", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Leadership, followership and teamwork", "x": "12. Behavioural"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b694", "k": "h", "t": "What is Communication?", "x": "What is Communication?"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b695", "k": "text", "t": "What is Communication?", "x": "Communication is the process of transmitting and receiving information: intentions, instructions, feelings or facts."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b696", "k": "text", "t": "What is Communication?", "x": "It requires: 1) a sender or transmitter; 2) a receiver; and 3) a message that travels between the two through 4) a medium or channel."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b697", "k": "text", "t": "What is Communication?", "x": "That medium can be our voice, hand signals, a text message, email or a whistle - anything that gets the message across."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b700", "k": "h", "t": "What is noise?", "x": "What is noise?"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b701", "k": "text", "t": "What is noise?", "x": "Every medium is vulnerable to noise: anything that distorts, blocks or changes the message, such as background interference, stress, fatigue, distraction, assumptions or jargon."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b703", "k": "h", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "Good and Bad Communication"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b704", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "A large proportion of avoidable medical errors, patient harm and lawsuits (!) are caused by poor communication."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b706", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "Communication is not what you say, but what the other person understands."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b707", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "The quality of our communication depends on the quality of the transmission, reception, message and medium."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b708", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "Just because someone hears something, it does not mean they will process or understand the message."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b709", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "People are primed to receive information by how we make them feel, as well as by our non-verbal communication."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b710", "k": "text", "t": "Good and Bad Communication", "x": "Noisy environments or distracting circumstances reduce our ability to transmit, receive and process information."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b712", "k": "h", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Think well, Speak well, Write well"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b713", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "When you think, speak or write follow the ABC rule: Accuracy, Brevity, Clarity"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b714", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Have clarity of thought before you decide to communicate. What is the one point you are trying to get across?"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b715", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Think of the speed, clarity and tone of your voice."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b716", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Speak slowly, calmly and confidently."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b717", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Never write an email when angry or upset."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b718", "k": "text", "t": "Think well, Speak well, Write well", "x": "Remember the rules of good writing: short sentences, short paragraphs"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b720", "k": "tool", "t": "How do you improve communication?", "x": "Listen first and most; speak second and least. Do not interrupt. Use people's names. Keep eye contact; speak face to face. Meet in person or call rather use email in any situation where tone is important or people might misunderstand intention. Use open body language."}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b728", "k": "tool", "t": "Crisis communication or briefing", "x": "Use the NITS framework to communicate in a crisis: Nature of the problem Intentions: what you are going to do about it? Timeline Special instructions: what you would like others to do now"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "b735", "k": "tool", "t": "Closed Loop Communication", "x": "What is closed-loop communication? Closed-loop communication is the discipline of confirming receipt and understanding of a message by readback and verification, so that no instruction or action is misunderstood.  How does it work? Closed-loop communication: the receiver confirms what they heard (readback), and the sender checks that it was understood correctly (hearback). Example: During a ward round, the consultant says: \"Can you give 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones, please.\" The nurse replies: \"Just to confirm - 1 gram of paracetamol IV for Mrs Jones in bed 4?\" (readback) The consultant responds: \"That's correct.\" (hearback) The nurse administers the drug and later reports back: \"Paracetamol 1 gram IV given at 14:00, Mrs Jones in bed 4.\" (confirmation that the action is complete)"}, {"p": "communication", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Communication", "x": "Communication"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b751", "k": "h", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "What are professional standards?"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b752", "k": "text", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "The behavioural hallmarks of what good looks like."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b754", "k": "text", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "“It must be done right!”"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b755", "k": "text", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "Robert Linton"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b756", "k": "text", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "Professionalism is about how you respect yourself, others and your work."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b758", "k": "text", "t": "What are professional standards?", "x": "As a professional, you are committed to maintaining the high standard."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b760", "k": "tool", "t": "Hallmarks of professional work", "x": "Do it properly (do it right) Do it all (do it in the right amount) Do it on time  Do it consistently (do it over time)"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b766", "k": "h", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "What does professionalism look like?"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b767", "k": "h", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "The five Cs"}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b768", "k": "text", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "Competence - The technical ability to do the work to standard."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b769", "k": "text", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "Condition - Turning up fit to do the job."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b770", "k": "text", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "Civility - How you treat people while you do it. Rudeness measurably degrades team performance."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b771", "k": "text", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "Conviction - Doing it the same way even when it is inconvenient, unobserved or unrewarded."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b772", "k": "text", "t": "What does professionalism look like?", "x": "Comportment – how you choose to carry yourself and behave towards others."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "b774", "k": "note", "t": "Why civility sits in a safety document", "x": "Incivility damages psychological safety and causes emotional trauma, A team that has been spoken to badly asks fewer questions, offers fewer challenges and reports fewer concerns."}, {"p": "professional", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Professional standards", "x": "Professional standards"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b781", "k": "h", "t": "Summary", "x": "Summary"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b782", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "‘Human factors’ is the science of human performance."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b783", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "It is understanding how we function and fail, and doing our best to avoid, trap, and mitigate error."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b784", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "It is about making the most of what we have to get what we need."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b786", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Human performance is affected by five factors:"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b787", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Technical and non-technical (human factors) skills."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b788", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Operator condition: physical, cognitive and emotional."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b789", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Culture (personal and organisational): values, attitudes, habits and identities."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b790", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Systems: the people, places, tools and rules available to do work."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b791", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "Task: complexity, size and urgency"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b793", "k": "text", "t": "Summary", "x": "There are three parts to human factors:"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b794", "k": "h", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Technical considerations"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b795", "k": "text", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Technology"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b796", "k": "text", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Tools extend the ability to do work."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b797", "k": "text", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Technology also adds complexity and increases the risk of errors occurring"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b798", "k": "text", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Safety by design and ergonomics improve how humans interact with machines."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b800", "k": "text", "t": "Technical considerations", "x": "Technical skills refer to an individual's knowledge, experience, and physical ability to do work."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b802", "k": "h", "t": "2. Cognitive skills", "x": "2. Cognitive skills"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b803", "k": "text", "t": "2. Cognitive skills", "x": "Workload management: Offload tasks appropriately. Apply the sterile cockpit and use dual operators."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b805", "k": "text", "t": "2. Cognitive skills", "x": "Situational awareness: Notice, understand, think ahead (NUTa). Protect the cognitive capacity."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b807", "k": "text", "t": "2. Cognitive skills", "x": "Problem solving: Create Cognitive Capacity → EOP → T-DODAR → first principles."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b809", "k": "h", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "3. Behavioural skills"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b810", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Leadership is the ability to drive positive change for those we serve;"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b811", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Lead by example"}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b812", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Take responsibility."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b813", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Be humbly confident."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b814", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "You can’t be a leader if you can’t work as a team member and follow others (even if they have a less senior hierarchical role than you)."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b816", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Communication: Keep things short, clear and specific. Listen first, speak second. Remember: body language, tone and eye contact."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "b818", "k": "text", "t": "3. Behavioural skills", "x": "Professional standards: Maintain the high standard and demand it of yourself and others."}, {"p": "summary", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "Summary", "x": "Summary of Human Factors"}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b840", "k": "glossary", "t": "Authority", "x": "How we choose to use the power that our hierarchical position gives us."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b841", "k": "glossary", "t": "Barriers", "x": "Anything that stops or avoids a threat from becoming an error."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b842", "k": "glossary", "t": "Biases", "x": "Shortcuts our brain uses to make quick decisions; they aren’t always right and can get us into trouble."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b843", "k": "glossary", "t": "Bow-tie analysis", "x": "A simple way of looking at the cause and effect of any hazard or failure - what leads into an error on one side, and what is the impact (harms) on the other."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b844", "k": "glossary", "t": "Closed-loop communication", "x": "The discipline of confirming receipt and understanding of a message by readback and verification, so that no instruction or action is misunderstood."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b845", "k": "glossary", "t": "Cognitive capacity", "x": "Your mental “bandwidth”."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b846", "k": "glossary", "t": "Communication", "x": "The process of transmitting and receiving information: intentions, instructions, feelings or facts."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b847", "k": "glossary", "t": "Crises", "x": "Situations where harms have not been correctly mitigated, leading to novel, uncertain and time-critical situations with multiple competing priorities."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b848", "k": "glossary", "t": "Culture", "x": "The values, attitudes and habits that shape how we feel, think and behave."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b849", "k": "glossary", "t": "Deliberate practice", "x": "Improves performance through repetition, feedback, and iterative refinement."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b850", "k": "glossary", "t": "Errors", "x": "Unintended outcomes - also known as system “failures”. Errors themselves don’t necessarily lead to harm."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b851", "k": "glossary", "t": "Harm", "x": "Errors that have negatively impacted someone or something, for example a patient, a member of staff or the organisation."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b852", "k": "glossary", "t": "Health system", "x": "Consists of four elements: people, places, tools and rules."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b853", "k": "glossary", "t": "Hierarchy", "x": "The role we play in an organisation. It is set and non-negotiable."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b854", "k": "glossary", "t": "Human factors", "x": "The science of optimising human performance to undertake complex tasks in fragile environments."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b855", "k": "glossary", "t": "Just culture", "x": "Accountability is attributed to individuals fairly and proportionally, with support, training or disciplinary action according to the nature of the error."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b856", "k": "glossary", "t": "Leadership", "x": "The ability to drive positive change for those we serve."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b857", "k": "glossary", "t": "Mitigations", "x": "Anything that stabilises a critical situation, reduces the impact of harm, and buys more time to fix a safety-critical problem."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b858", "k": "glossary", "t": "Noise", "x": "Anything that distorts, blocks or changes the message."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b859", "k": "glossary", "t": "Operator condition", "x": "Physical, cognitive and emotional status that limits our ability to perform tasks."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b860", "k": "glossary", "t": "Professional standards", "x": "The behavioural hallmarks of what good looks like."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b861", "k": "glossary", "t": "Safety by design", "x": "Make it easy for people to do things right and hard to do things wrong."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b862", "k": "glossary", "t": "Situational awareness", "x": "The ability to notice, understand and think ahead."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b863", "k": "glossary", "t": "Socio-technical interface", "x": "The interaction between a human and a machine."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b864", "k": "glossary", "t": "Startle", "x": "A reaction where your emotions take over your thinking. You stop being rational and logical."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b865", "k": "glossary", "t": "Sterile cockpit", "x": "An enforced distraction-free environment during critical or high-risk phases of work."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b866", "k": "glossary", "t": "Technical skills", "x": "An individual’s knowledge, experience, and physical ability to do work."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b867", "k": "glossary", "t": "Technology", "x": "The tools, equipment and innovations that extend human capability and address human needs."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b868", "k": "glossary", "t": "Threats", "x": "Any risk factors that make errors more likely; conditions that push towards failure."}, {"p": "glossary", "b": "b869", "k": "glossary", "t": "Traps", "x": "Anything that stops an error that has occurred from leading to harm."}, {"p": "references", "b": "b823", "k": "note", "t": "Original content", "x": "All original content in this guide is derived from the source below and should not be reproduced or shared without the author's permission. \"High Performance in Healthcare - Optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery.\" - P Gogalniceanu, King's College London, 2019 Delivered as Principles of Human Factors by Dr Peter Gogalniceanu (2026, all rights reserved)"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b829", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "1. Gogalniceanu P. High performance in healthcare: optimising systems, cultures and interventions in surgery [teaching material]. London: King’s College London; 2019."}, {"p": "references", "b": "b830", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "2. Wiegmann DA, Shappell SA. A human error analysis of commercial aviation accidents using the Human Factors Analysis and Classification System (HFACS). Washington (DC): Federal Aviation Administration, Office of Aviation Medicine; 2001. Report No.: DOT/FAA/AM-01/3. Available from: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/data_research/research/med_humanfacs/oamtechreports/0103.pdf"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b831", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "3. Smits M, Zegers M, Groenewegen PP, Timmermans DRM, Zwaan L, van der Wal G, et al. Exploring the causes of adverse events in hospitals and potential prevention strategies. Qual Saf Health Care. 2010;19(5):e5. doi:10.1136/qshc.2008.030726. https://doi.org/10.1136/qshc.2008.030726"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b832", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "4. Gogalniceanu P, Karydis N, Kessaris N, Olsburgh J, Calder F, Sevdalis N, et al. How crises work: a model of error cause and effect in surgical practice. Int J Surg. 2022;104:106711. doi:10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijsu.2022.106711"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b833", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "5. Gogalniceanu P, Karydis N, Loukopoulos I, Kessaris N, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Avoid, trap, and mitigate: development of an evidence-based crisis management framework in surgery. J Am Coll Surg. 2021;233(4):526-536.e1. doi:10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2021.06.010"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b834", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "6. Gogalniceanu P, Kaafarani H. Am I safe? Surgery. 2022;171(2):556-557. doi:10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surg.2021.10.007"}, {"p": "references", "b": "b835", "k": "text", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "7. Gogalniceanu P, Kunduzi B, Ruckley C, Kaafarani H, Sevdalis N, Mamode N. Surgical leadership in a culture of safety: an inter-professional study of metrics and tools for improving clinical practice. Am J Surg. 2024;228:32-42. doi:10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.amjsurg.2023.09.002"}, {"p": "references", "b": "", "k": "page", "t": "References and acknowledgement", "x": "References and acknowledgement"}]};
